/* AI brain Q&A pack 11: food & world cuisine */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["how do i make crepes | crepe recipe | how to make crepes | thin pancakes recipe", "Easy crepes (makes about 8):\n1. Blend or whisk 1 cup flour, 2 eggs, 1 1/4 cups milk, 2 tbsp melted butter and a pinch of salt until smooth.\n2. Let the batter rest 15 minutes.\n3. Heat a lightly buttered pan over medium heat and pour in about 1/4 cup, swirling it thin.\n4. Cook about 1 minute, flip, cook 30 seconds more, then fill with fruit, jam or cheese. Ask an adult to help at the stove."],
  ["how do i make a breakfast burrito | breakfast burrito recipe | egg burrito", "Breakfast burrito:\n1. Scramble 2 eggs in a pan.\n2. Warm a large flour tortilla in a dry pan or microwave for a few seconds.\n3. Fill it with the eggs, shredded cheese, and extras like cooked potatoes, beans, bacon or salsa.\n4. Fold in the sides, roll it up tightly and enjoy (or toast it seam-side down to seal it)."],
  ["how do i make avocado toast | avocado toast recipe | smashed avocado on toast", "Avocado toast:\n1. Toast a slice of good bread.\n2. Cut a ripe avocado in half, remove the pit and scoop the flesh into a bowl.\n3. Mash it with a fork plus a pinch of salt and a squeeze of lemon or lime.\n4. Spread on the toast and top with extras like chili flakes, a fried egg or sliced tomato."],
  ["how do i make overnight oats | overnight oats recipe | no cook oatmeal", "Overnight oats:\n1. In a jar, mix 1/2 cup rolled oats with 1/2 cup milk (any kind) and 1/4 cup yogurt.\n2. Add a little honey or maple syrup and a pinch of cinnamon.\n3. Stir, cover and refrigerate overnight (at least 4 hours).\n4. In the morning, top with fruit, nuts or seeds and eat it cold."],
  ["how do i make granola | homemade granola | granola recipe", "Homemade granola:\n1. Mix 3 cups rolled oats, 1 cup chopped nuts or seeds, 1/2 tsp cinnamon and a pinch of salt.\n2. Stir in 1/3 cup honey or maple syrup and 1/4 cup melted coconut oil or vegetable oil.\n3. Spread on a lined baking sheet and bake at 325°F (160°C) for 20–25 minutes, stirring halfway.\n4. Let it cool completely (it crisps as it cools), then add dried fruit and store in a sealed jar."],
  ["how do i make shakshuka | shakshuka recipe | eggs in tomato sauce", "Shakshuka (eggs poached in spiced tomato sauce):\n1. Cook a chopped onion and a sliced bell pepper in olive oil until soft; add 2 cloves garlic, 1 tsp cumin and 1 tsp paprika.\n2. Pour in a can of crushed tomatoes and simmer 10 minutes until thick; season with salt.\n3. Make little wells and crack 4 eggs into them.\n4. Cover and cook on low 5–8 minutes until the whites are set. Serve with bread. Ask an adult for help with the stove."],
  ["how do i make a frittata | frittata recipe | baked egg dish", "Easy frittata:\n1. Heat the oven to 375°F (190°C).\n2. Whisk 8 eggs with 1/4 cup milk, salt and pepper.\n3. Soften some veggies (spinach, peppers, onion) in an oven-safe pan with a little oil, then pour in the eggs and add cheese.\n4. Cook on the stove 3–4 minutes until the edges set, then bake 10–15 minutes until the center is firm. Use oven mitts — the handle gets hot!"],
  ["how do i make egg muffins | egg cups recipe | breakfast egg bites | muffin tin eggs", "Egg muffins:\n1. Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C) and grease a 12-cup muffin tin.\n2. Whisk 10 eggs with a splash of milk, salt and pepper.\n3. Divide chopped veggies, cooked ham or sausage and cheese among the cups, then pour the egg on top.\n4. Bake 18–22 minutes until set in the middle. They keep in the fridge for 3–4 days."],
  ["how do i make hash browns | hash brown recipe | crispy shredded potatoes", "Crispy hash browns:\n1. Grate 2 peeled potatoes (ask an adult — graters are sharp!).\n2. Squeeze out as much water as you can using a clean towel.\n3. Season with salt and spread in a thin layer in a hot pan with plenty of oil.\n4. Cook without stirring 5–6 minutes until golden, flip, and cook the other side."],
  ["how do i make chia pudding | chia seed pudding | chia pudding recipe", "Chia pudding:\n1. Stir 3 tbsp chia seeds into 1 cup milk (dairy, almond, coconut — any works).\n2. Add 1–2 tsp honey or maple syrup and a little vanilla.\n3. Stir again after 5 minutes so it doesn't clump.\n4. Refrigerate at least 2 hours or overnight until thick, then top with fruit."],
  ["how do i make banana pancakes | 2 ingredient pancakes | banana egg pancakes", "Two-ingredient banana pancakes:\n1. Mash 1 ripe banana really well.\n2. Whisk in 2 eggs until smooth (add a pinch of cinnamon if you like).\n3. Pour small 2–3 inch circles into a buttered pan over medium-low heat.\n4. Cook about 1–2 minutes per side — they're delicate, so flip gently."],
  ["how do i make a breakfast sandwich | egg mcmuffin at home | egg and cheese sandwich", "Breakfast sandwich:\n1. Toast an English muffin or bagel.\n2. Fry an egg (break the yolk if you want it neat), adding a slice of cheese on top for the last minute so it melts.\n3. Warm a slice of ham, bacon or a sausage patty.\n4. Stack everything on the muffin and enjoy."],
  ["how do i make cinnamon toast | cinnamon sugar toast | cinnamon toast recipe", "Cinnamon toast:\n1. Mix 2 tbsp sugar with 1 tsp cinnamon.\n2. Toast a slice of bread.\n3. Spread it with softened butter right away.\n4. Sprinkle the cinnamon sugar all over and eat while it's warm."],
  ["how do i make biscuits | homemade biscuits | buttermilk biscuits recipe | fluffy biscuits", "Buttermilk biscuits:\n1. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C). Mix 2 cups flour, 1 tbsp baking powder, 1/2 tsp baking soda and 1 tsp salt.\n2. Cut in 6 tbsp very cold butter until it looks like coarse crumbs.\n3. Stir in about 3/4 cup cold buttermilk just until a shaggy dough forms.\n4. Pat to 1 inch thick, cut rounds and bake 12–15 minutes until golden."],
  ["how do i make scones | scone recipe | easy scones", "Simple scones:\n1. Heat the oven to 400°F (200°C). Mix 2 cups flour, 1/4 cup sugar, 1 tbsp baking powder and 1/2 tsp salt.\n2. Rub in 6 tbsp cold butter until crumbly, then stir in 2/3 cup cream or milk and 1 egg (plus raisins or berries if you like).\n3. Pat into a 1-inch-thick circle and cut into 8 wedges.\n4. Bake 15–18 minutes until golden. Serve with jam."],
  ["how do i make huevos rancheros | huevos rancheros recipe", "Huevos rancheros (Mexican ranch-style eggs):\n1. Warm corn tortillas in a lightly oiled pan.\n2. Fry 2 eggs.\n3. Spread warm refried beans on the tortillas and put the eggs on top.\n4. Spoon over warm salsa and add avocado, cheese and cilantro."],
  ["how do i make congee | rice porridge recipe | jook", "Congee (savory rice porridge):\n1. Rinse 1/2 cup rice and put it in a pot with 6 cups water or broth.\n2. Add a few slices of ginger and bring to a boil.\n3. Lower the heat and simmer 1–1.5 hours, stirring now and then, until thick and creamy.\n4. Season with salt or soy sauce and top with green onions, an egg or shredded chicken."],
  ["how do i make a dutch baby | dutch baby pancake | german pancake", "Dutch baby (puffy oven pancake):\n1. Put 2 tbsp butter in a cast-iron or oven-safe pan and heat it in a 425°F (220°C) oven.\n2. Blend 3 eggs, 1/2 cup flour, 1/2 cup milk, 1 tbsp sugar and a pinch of salt.\n3. Carefully pour the batter into the hot pan (adult job — it's very hot!).\n4. Bake 18–20 minutes until puffed and golden. Top with powdered sugar and lemon."],
  ["how do i make a poached egg | poached egg recipe | how to poach an egg", "Poached egg:\n1. Bring a pot of water to a gentle simmer (small bubbles, not a rolling boil) and add a splash of vinegar.\n2. Crack a fresh egg into a small cup.\n3. Stir the water to make a gentle swirl and slide the egg into the center.\n4. Cook 3–4 minutes, then lift it out with a slotted spoon. Ask an adult to help with the hot water."],
  ["how do i make yogurt at home | homemade yogurt | yogurt recipe", "Homemade yogurt:\n1. Heat 4 cups milk to about 180°F (82°C), then let it cool to about 110°F (43°C).\n2. Stir in 2 tbsp plain yogurt that has live cultures.\n3. Keep it warm (in an oven with just the light on, or a yogurt maker) for 6–12 hours until thick.\n4. Refrigerate. Save 2 tbsp to start your next batch!"],
  ["how do i make tuna salad | tuna sandwich | tuna salad recipe | tuna mayo", "Tuna salad:\n1. Drain a can of tuna and flake it into a bowl.\n2. Mix in 2–3 tbsp mayonnaise, a little chopped celery or pickle, and a squeeze of lemon.\n3. Season with salt and pepper.\n4. Serve on bread, crackers or lettuce leaves."],
  ["how do i make egg salad | egg salad sandwich | egg mayo", "Egg salad:\n1. Hard-boil 4 eggs, cool them and peel.\n2. Chop them up in a bowl.\n3. Mix with 2–3 tbsp mayo, 1 tsp mustard, salt and pepper (chives are nice too).\n4. Spread on bread or serve in lettuce cups."],
  ["how do i make chicken salad | chicken salad recipe | chicken salad sandwich", "Chicken salad:\n1. Chop or shred 2 cups cooked chicken.\n2. Mix with 1/3 cup mayo or Greek yogurt and a spoon of mustard.\n3. Stir in chopped celery, grapes or apple for crunch.\n4. Season with salt and pepper and serve on bread or croissants."],
  ["how do i make a blt | blt sandwich | bacon lettuce tomato sandwich", "BLT sandwich:\n1. Cook 3–4 slices of bacon until crisp and drain on a paper towel.\n2. Toast 2 slices of bread and spread with mayo.\n3. Layer lettuce, sliced tomato (sprinkled with a pinch of salt) and bacon.\n4. Top with the other slice, cut in half and enjoy."],
  ["how do i make a wrap | wrap recipe | chicken wrap | lunch wrap ideas", "Easy wrap:\n1. Lay a large tortilla flat and spread with hummus, cream cheese or a sauce.\n2. Add a protein like sliced turkey, chicken, beans or falafel.\n3. Add crunchy veggies (lettuce, cucumber, carrot, peppers) and cheese.\n4. Fold in the sides, roll it up tightly and slice in half."],
  ["how do i make caesar salad | caesar salad recipe", "Caesar salad:\n1. Chop romaine lettuce and put it in a big bowl.\n2. Whisk a quick dressing: 1/3 cup mayo, 2 tbsp grated parmesan, 1 tbsp lemon juice, 1 tsp Dijon mustard, 1 small grated garlic clove and black pepper (anchovy paste is traditional).\n3. Toss the lettuce with the dressing.\n4. Top with croutons and more parmesan."],
  ["how do i make greek salad | greek salad recipe | horiatiki", "Greek salad (horiatiki):\n1. Cut tomatoes and cucumber into chunks and thinly slice some red onion.\n2. Add Kalamata olives and a slab or crumbles of feta cheese.\n3. Drizzle with olive oil and a splash of red wine vinegar.\n4. Sprinkle with dried oregano and a pinch of salt. Traditionally it has no lettuce!"],
  ["how do i make caprese salad | caprese recipe | tomato mozzarella basil salad", "Caprese salad:\n1. Slice ripe tomatoes and fresh mozzarella into rounds.\n2. Arrange them on a plate, alternating, with fresh basil leaves.\n3. Drizzle with good olive oil and sprinkle with salt and pepper.\n4. Add a little balsamic glaze if you like. Its red, white and green match the Italian flag!"],
  ["how do i make tomato soup | tomato soup recipe | homemade tomato soup", "Tomato soup:\n1. Soften a chopped onion and 2 garlic cloves in butter or olive oil.\n2. Add a 28 oz (800 g) can of tomatoes and 2 cups broth; simmer 15–20 minutes.\n3. Blend until smooth (let it cool a bit first and have an adult help — hot liquids can splatter).\n4. Stir in a splash of cream, season with salt and pepper, and serve with grilled cheese."],
  ["how do i make chicken noodle soup | chicken noodle soup recipe | soup for a cold", "Chicken noodle soup:\n1. Cook chopped onion, carrots and celery in a little oil until soft.\n2. Add 8 cups chicken broth and bring to a boil.\n3. Add 2 cups egg noodles and cook 6–8 minutes.\n4. Stir in 2 cups cooked shredded chicken, season with salt, pepper and parsley, and heat through."],
  ["how do i make minestrone | minestrone soup recipe | italian vegetable soup", "Minestrone:\n1. Soften chopped onion, carrot, celery and garlic in olive oil.\n2. Add diced zucchini, a can of tomatoes, 6 cups broth and a can of beans; simmer 15 minutes.\n3. Add a handful of small pasta and cook until tender.\n4. Stir in spinach, season, and serve with parmesan on top."],
  ["how do i make lentil soup | lentil soup recipe", "Lentil soup:\n1. Soften chopped onion, carrot and garlic in olive oil; add 1 tsp cumin.\n2. Add 1 cup rinsed lentils, a can of tomatoes and 6 cups broth.\n3. Simmer 25–30 minutes until the lentils are soft.\n4. Season with salt, pepper and a squeeze of lemon."],
  ["how do i make pasta salad | pasta salad recipe | cold pasta salad", "Pasta salad:\n1. Cook short pasta (like rotini), drain and rinse with cold water.\n2. Add chopped veggies: cherry tomatoes, cucumber, bell pepper, olives.\n3. Add cubes of mozzarella or feta.\n4. Toss with Italian dressing or olive oil and vinegar, then chill before serving."],
  ["how do i make potato salad | potato salad recipe", "Classic potato salad:\n1. Boil 2 lb (900 g) cubed potatoes in salted water 10–12 minutes until just tender; drain and cool.\n2. Mix 1/2 cup mayo, 1 tbsp mustard, a splash of vinegar, salt and pepper.\n3. Fold in the potatoes with chopped celery, green onion and chopped hard-boiled eggs.\n4. Chill at least an hour. Keep it cold at picnics!"],
  ["how do i make coleslaw | coleslaw recipe | cole slaw", "Coleslaw:\n1. Shred half a cabbage and 1 carrot (or use a bag of coleslaw mix).\n2. Whisk 1/2 cup mayo, 1 tbsp vinegar, 1 tsp sugar, salt and pepper.\n3. Toss the cabbage with the dressing.\n4. Chill 30 minutes so the flavors blend."],
  ["how do i make sloppy joes | sloppy joe recipe", "Sloppy joes:\n1. Brown 1 lb (450 g) ground beef with a chopped onion; drain the fat.\n2. Stir in 3/4 cup ketchup, 1 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tsp mustard and a splash of Worcestershire sauce.\n3. Simmer 10 minutes until thick.\n4. Spoon onto toasted burger buns — it's supposed to be messy!"],
  ["how do i make nachos | nachos recipe | loaded nachos | sheet pan nachos", "Sheet pan nachos:\n1. Heat the oven to 400°F (200°C).\n2. Spread tortilla chips on a baking sheet.\n3. Top with shredded cheese, beans or cooked ground meat, and jalapeños if you like.\n4. Bake 6–8 minutes until melted, then add salsa, sour cream and guacamole."],
  ["how do i make fried rice | fried rice recipe | egg fried rice", "Fried rice:\n1. Use cold, day-old cooked rice (fresh rice gets mushy).\n2. Scramble 2 eggs in a hot oiled pan and set them aside.\n3. Stir-fry chopped veggies (peas, carrots, onion), then add the rice and stir until hot.\n4. Add soy sauce and the eggs back in, toss, and top with green onions. Ask an adult for help at the stove."],
  ["how do i make english muffin pizzas | mini pizzas | bagel pizza", "Mini English muffin pizzas:\n1. Split English muffins in half and toast them lightly.\n2. Spread with pizza sauce.\n3. Sprinkle with mozzarella and your favorite toppings.\n4. Bake at 400°F (200°C) for 8–10 minutes or toaster-oven until bubbly."],
  ["how do i make a club sandwich | club sandwich recipe", "Club sandwich:\n1. Toast 3 slices of bread and spread with mayo.\n2. Layer slice 1 with turkey and lettuce.\n3. Add slice 2, then bacon and tomato, and top with slice 3.\n4. Cut into 4 triangles and hold each together with a toothpick."],
  ["how do i make a cucumber sandwich | cucumber sandwiches | tea sandwiches", "Cucumber tea sandwiches:\n1. Thinly slice a cucumber and pat the slices dry.\n2. Spread soft white bread with cream cheese or butter.\n3. Layer cucumber, sprinkle with salt and a little dill.\n4. Top with bread, cut off the crusts and cut into small triangles or fingers."],
  ["how do i make hummus | hummus recipe | homemade hummus", "Homemade hummus:\n1. Drain a can of chickpeas (save a little liquid).\n2. Blend with 1/4 cup tahini, juice of 1 lemon, 1 garlic clove and a pinch of salt.\n3. Add 2–4 tbsp cold water or chickpea liquid and blend until very smooth.\n4. Spread in a bowl, drizzle with olive oil and sprinkle with paprika."],
  ["how do i make falafel | falafel recipe | homemade falafel", "Falafel:\n1. Soak 1 cup dried chickpeas overnight (canned ones are too wet), then drain.\n2. Pulse in a food processor with onion, garlic, parsley, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp coriander and salt until coarse.\n3. Shape into small balls or patties.\n4. Bake at 400°F (200°C) for 25 minutes, flipping halfway, or have an adult fry them. Serve in pita with tahini sauce."],
  ["how do i make meatballs | meatball recipe | homemade meatballs", "Meatballs:\n1. Mix 1 lb (450 g) ground meat, 1/2 cup breadcrumbs, 1 egg, 1/4 cup parmesan, minced garlic, salt and pepper.\n2. Roll into 1.5-inch balls (wash your hands after touching raw meat!).\n3. Bake at 400°F (200°C) for 18–20 minutes until cooked through (160°F / 71°C inside).\n4. Simmer them in tomato sauce and serve with spaghetti."],
  ["how do i make lasagna | lasagna recipe | easy lasagna", "Easy lasagna:\n1. Brown 1 lb ground beef or sausage and mix with a jar of pasta sauce.\n2. Mix 2 cups ricotta with 1 egg and 1/2 cup parmesan.\n3. In a 9x13 dish, layer sauce, lasagna noodles (no-boil type is easiest), ricotta and mozzarella; repeat 3 times, ending with sauce and mozzarella.\n4. Cover with foil and bake at 375°F (190°C) for 45 minutes, uncover for 10 more, then rest 15 minutes before cutting."],
  ["how do i make chili | chili recipe | chili con carne | easy chili", "Easy chili:\n1. Brown 1 lb ground beef with a chopped onion and 2 garlic cloves; drain the fat.\n2. Stir in 2 tbsp chili powder and 1 tsp cumin.\n3. Add a can of diced tomatoes, a can of tomato sauce and 2 cans of drained beans.\n4. Simmer 30 minutes, season with salt, and top with cheese and sour cream."],
  ["how do i make beef stew | beef stew recipe | stew", "Beef stew:\n1. Season 2 lb cubed stew beef, dust with flour and brown it in batches in a hot oiled pot.\n2. Add chopped onion and garlic, then 4 cups beef broth and 2 tbsp tomato paste.\n3. Cover and simmer gently 1.5 hours.\n4. Add chunks of potato and carrot and cook 30–45 minutes more until everything is tender."],
  ["how do i roast a chicken | roast chicken recipe | whole roasted chicken", "Roast chicken:\n1. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C). Pat a whole chicken dry with paper towels.\n2. Rub with oil or butter and season well with salt and pepper (put lemon and garlic inside if you like).\n3. Roast about 1 hour to 1.5 hours (roughly 20 minutes per pound) until the thickest part of the thigh reads **165°F (74°C)**.\n4. Rest 10–15 minutes before carving. Wash hands and surfaces after handling raw chicken."],
  ["how do i bake salmon | baked salmon recipe | how to cook salmon in the oven", "Baked salmon:\n1. Heat the oven to 400°F (200°C) and put salmon fillets on a lined baking sheet.\n2. Brush with olive oil and season with salt, pepper, garlic and lemon slices.\n3. Bake 12–15 minutes until it flakes easily with a fork.\n4. The safe internal temperature for fish is **145°F (63°C)**."],
  ["how do i make fish tacos | fish taco recipe", "Fish tacos:\n1. Season white fish fillets (like cod or tilapia) with chili powder, cumin, salt and lime.\n2. Bake at 400°F (200°C) for 10–12 minutes or pan-cook until flaky.\n3. Make a quick slaw of shredded cabbage with lime juice and a little mayo.\n4. Flake the fish into warm tortillas, add slaw, salsa and avocado."],
  ["how do i make fajitas | chicken fajitas recipe | fajita recipe", "Chicken fajitas:\n1. Slice chicken breasts, bell peppers and an onion into strips.\n2. Toss the chicken with oil, lime juice, chili powder, cumin, garlic powder and salt.\n3. Cook the chicken in a very hot pan until done, then cook the peppers and onions until slightly charred.\n4. Serve sizzling in warm tortillas with salsa, sour cream and guacamole."],
  ["how do i make a burrito bowl | burrito bowl recipe | chipotle bowl at home", "Burrito bowl:\n1. Start with a scoop of rice (try mixing in lime juice and cilantro).\n2. Add black or pinto beans and a protein like seasoned chicken, beef or tofu.\n3. Pile on corn, salsa, lettuce and cheese.\n4. Finish with guacamole or sour cream."],
  ["how do i make chicken curry | easy chicken curry | curry recipe", "Easy chicken curry:\n1. Soften a chopped onion in oil, then add 2 garlic cloves, 1 tbsp grated ginger and 2 tbsp curry powder; stir 1 minute.\n2. Add 1.5 lb (680 g) bite-size chicken pieces and cook until no longer pink outside.\n3. Pour in a can of coconut milk and a can of diced tomatoes; simmer 20 minutes.\n4. Season with salt and serve with rice or naan."],
  ["how do i make dal | dal recipe | dhal | indian lentils", "Simple dal:\n1. Rinse 1 cup red lentils and simmer with 3 cups water and 1/2 tsp turmeric for 20 minutes until soft.\n2. In a small pan, heat oil and cook 1 tsp cumin seeds until they sizzle, then add chopped onion, garlic and ginger.\n3. Stir in a chopped tomato and cook until soft.\n4. Pour this mixture into the lentils, season with salt and serve with rice or roti."],
  ["how do i make pad thai | pad thai recipe | easy pad thai", "Simple pad thai:\n1. Soak 8 oz flat rice noodles in hot water until soft, then drain.\n2. Mix a sauce: 3 tbsp fish sauce (or soy sauce), 2 tbsp brown sugar, 2 tbsp tamarind paste or lime juice.\n3. Stir-fry garlic and chicken, shrimp or tofu in oil; push aside and scramble 2 eggs.\n4. Add the noodles and sauce, toss, then add bean sprouts and green onions. Top with peanuts and lime."],
  ["how do i make teriyaki chicken | teriyaki chicken recipe | homemade teriyaki sauce", "Teriyaki chicken:\n1. Mix 1/2 cup soy sauce, 1/4 cup water, 3 tbsp brown sugar or honey, 1 tsp grated ginger and 1 garlic clove.\n2. Cook bite-size chicken pieces in a hot oiled pan until cooked through.\n3. Pour in the sauce and simmer, stirring, until it thickens and coats the chicken (add 1 tsp cornstarch mixed with water to thicken faster).\n4. Serve over rice with sesame seeds and green onions."],
  ["how do i make shepherd's pie | shepherds pie recipe | cottage pie", "Shepherd's pie:\n1. Brown 1 lb ground lamb (shepherd's pie) or beef (cottage pie) with chopped onion and carrot.\n2. Add 1 tbsp tomato paste, 1 cup broth and a cup of peas; simmer until thick.\n3. Spread in a baking dish and top with a thick layer of mashed potatoes.\n4. Bake at 400°F (200°C) for 20–25 minutes until the top is golden."],
  ["how do i make meatloaf | meatloaf recipe", "Meatloaf:\n1. Mix 2 lb ground beef, 1 cup breadcrumbs, 2 eggs, 1/2 cup milk, a chopped onion, salt and pepper.\n2. Shape into a loaf in a pan or on a lined baking sheet.\n3. Spread ketchup (mixed with a little brown sugar) on top.\n4. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for about 1 hour until it reaches **160°F (71°C)** inside. Rest 10 minutes before slicing."],
  ["how do i make pot roast | pot roast recipe", "Pot roast:\n1. Season a 3 lb chuck roast with salt and pepper and brown it on all sides in a hot oiled pot.\n2. Add an onion, garlic and 2 cups beef broth.\n3. Cover and cook in a 300°F (150°C) oven for about 3 hours.\n4. Add potatoes and carrots for the last hour. It's done when it falls apart easily with a fork."],
  ["how do i cook pork chops | pork chop recipe | pan fried pork chops", "Pan-seared pork chops:\n1. Pat chops dry and season with salt, pepper and garlic powder.\n2. Sear in a hot oiled pan 3–5 minutes per side until golden.\n3. Cook until the inside reaches **145°F (63°C)**, then rest 3 minutes.\n4. Serve with applesauce, a classic pairing."],
  ["how do i make baked ziti | baked ziti recipe", "Baked ziti:\n1. Cook 1 lb ziti or penne a couple of minutes short of done; drain.\n2. Mix with a jar of marinara, 1 cup ricotta and half of 2 cups shredded mozzarella.\n3. Pour into a baking dish and top with the rest of the mozzarella and some parmesan.\n4. Bake at 375°F (190°C) for 20–25 minutes until bubbly."],
  ["how do i make alfredo sauce | fettuccine alfredo recipe | alfredo pasta", "Fettuccine Alfredo:\n1. Cook fettuccine in salted water and save a cup of the pasta water.\n2. Melt 4 tbsp butter in a pan and add 1 cup heavy cream; warm gently.\n3. Stir in 1 cup finely grated parmesan until smooth.\n4. Toss with the pasta, adding splashes of pasta water to loosen; season with pepper. (The original Roman version is just butter, parmesan and pasta water!)"],
  ["how do i make pesto | pesto recipe | basil pesto", "Basil pesto:\n1. Put 2 cups fresh basil leaves, 1/3 cup pine nuts (or walnuts), 1 garlic clove and 1/2 cup parmesan in a food processor.\n2. Pulse until finely chopped.\n3. With the machine running, pour in about 1/2 cup olive oil.\n4. Season with salt and toss with pasta. Pesto comes from Genoa, Italy."],
  ["how do i make carbonara | spaghetti carbonara recipe | carbonara", "Spaghetti carbonara:\n1. Whisk 2 eggs + 2 yolks with 3/4 cup grated pecorino or parmesan and lots of black pepper.\n2. Crisp 4 oz diced guanciale, pancetta or bacon in a pan; turn off the heat.\n3. Add hot drained spaghetti to the pan and toss.\n4. Off the heat, pour in the egg mixture with a splash of pasta water and toss quickly so it turns creamy (not scrambled). Traditional carbonara has no cream!"],
  ["how do i make garlic pasta | aglio e olio | spaghetti with garlic and oil", "Spaghetti aglio e olio:\n1. Cook spaghetti in salted water; save a cup of pasta water.\n2. Gently cook 4–6 sliced garlic cloves in 1/3 cup olive oil over low heat until pale golden (don't let it burn).\n3. Add a pinch of chili flakes, then the drained pasta and a splash of pasta water.\n4. Toss well, then add chopped parsley and parmesan if you like."],
  ["how do i make risotto | risotto recipe | creamy risotto", "Basic risotto:\n1. Keep 4 cups broth warm in a pot. Soften a chopped onion in butter.\n2. Add 1 cup arborio rice and stir 1–2 minutes until the grains look glassy at the edges.\n3. Add warm broth one ladle at a time, stirring often and waiting until each is mostly absorbed (about 18–20 minutes).\n4. When creamy and just tender, stir in butter and parmesan and season."],
  ["how do i make jambalaya | jambalaya recipe", "Jambalaya:\n1. Brown sliced smoked sausage and bite-size chicken in a big pot.\n2. Add the 'holy trinity' — chopped onion, celery and bell pepper — plus garlic and Cajun seasoning.\n3. Stir in 1.5 cups rice, a can of diced tomatoes and 3 cups broth.\n4. Cover and simmer 20–25 minutes until the rice is tender. Add shrimp in the last 5 minutes if you like."],
  ["how do i make chicken parmesan | chicken parm recipe | chicken parmigiana", "Chicken parmesan:\n1. Pound chicken breasts thin, dip in beaten egg, then in breadcrumbs mixed with parmesan.\n2. Pan-fry in oil until golden on both sides (or bake at 425°F / 220°C for about 20 minutes).\n3. Top with marinara and mozzarella.\n4. Bake until the cheese melts and the chicken reaches **165°F (74°C)**. Serve with spaghetti."],
  ["how do i make chicken nuggets | homemade chicken nuggets | chicken tenders recipe", "Baked chicken nuggets:\n1. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C) and cut chicken breast into bite-size pieces.\n2. Dip each piece in beaten egg, then roll in breadcrumbs or crushed cornflakes mixed with salt and paprika.\n3. Place on a greased baking sheet and spray or drizzle with a little oil.\n4. Bake 15–18 minutes, flipping halfway, until golden and cooked through. Wash hands after touching raw chicken!"],
  ["how do i make stuffed peppers | stuffed bell peppers recipe", "Stuffed peppers:\n1. Cut the tops off 4 bell peppers and remove the seeds.\n2. Mix cooked rice, cooked ground beef or beans, tomato sauce, seasoning and some cheese.\n3. Stuff the peppers and stand them in a baking dish with a little water in the bottom.\n4. Cover and bake at 375°F (190°C) for 35–45 minutes, topping with more cheese at the end."],
  ["how do i make enchiladas | enchilada recipe | chicken enchiladas", "Chicken enchiladas:\n1. Mix 2 cups shredded cooked chicken with 1 cup shredded cheese and a little enchilada sauce.\n2. Roll the filling up in tortillas and lay them seam-side down in a baking dish.\n3. Pour enchilada sauce over the top and sprinkle with more cheese.\n4. Bake at 375°F (190°C) for 20 minutes until bubbly."],
  ["how do i make a sheet pan dinner | sheet pan chicken and vegetables | one pan dinner", "Sheet pan chicken dinner:\n1. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C).\n2. Toss chicken thighs and chunks of potato, carrot and broccoli with oil, salt, pepper and garlic powder.\n3. Spread everything in one layer on a big sheet pan (don't crowd it).\n4. Roast 30–35 minutes until the chicken reaches 165°F (74°C) and the veggies are browned."],
  ["how do i make black bean burgers | veggie burger recipe | bean burger", "Black bean burgers:\n1. Mash a drained can of black beans (leave some chunky) and pat dry first so they're not too wet.\n2. Mix in 1/2 cup breadcrumbs, 1 egg, 1/4 cup finely chopped onion, 1 tsp cumin and salt.\n3. Shape into 4 patties and chill 15 minutes.\n4. Cook in an oiled pan 4–5 minutes per side until crisp, and serve on buns."],
  ["how do i make butter chicken | butter chicken recipe | murgh makhani", "Butter chicken:\n1. Marinate bite-size chicken in yogurt, garlic, ginger and garam masala for at least 30 minutes.\n2. Cook the chicken in a pan until browned and cooked through; set aside.\n3. In butter, cook onion, garlic and ginger, then add tomato puree, garam masala, a little chili and salt; simmer 10 minutes.\n4. Stir in cream and the chicken, simmer a few minutes and serve with rice or naan."],
  ["how do i make beef and broccoli | beef and broccoli recipe", "Beef and broccoli:\n1. Slice 1 lb flank steak thinly against the grain and toss with 1 tbsp cornstarch and 1 tbsp soy sauce.\n2. Stir-fry the beef in a very hot oiled pan until browned; set aside.\n3. Stir-fry broccoli florets with a splash of water until bright green and crisp-tender.\n4. Add back the beef plus a sauce of soy sauce, oyster sauce, brown sugar and garlic; toss until glossy. Serve over rice."],
  ["how do i make sweet and sour chicken | sweet and sour sauce recipe", "Sweet and sour chicken:\n1. Coat bite-size chicken in cornstarch and pan-fry until golden and cooked.\n2. Mix a sauce: 1/2 cup pineapple juice, 1/4 cup vinegar, 3 tbsp ketchup, 3 tbsp sugar and 1 tbsp soy sauce.\n3. Stir-fry chunks of bell pepper, onion and pineapple, then add the sauce and simmer.\n4. Thicken with 1 tsp cornstarch in water, toss in the chicken and serve with rice."],
  ["how do i make lo mein | lo mein recipe | chinese noodles", "Lo mein:\n1. Cook lo mein or spaghetti noodles and drain.\n2. Mix a sauce of 3 tbsp soy sauce, 1 tbsp oyster sauce, 1 tsp sesame oil and 1 tsp sugar.\n3. Stir-fry sliced veggies (cabbage, carrots, mushrooms) and a protein in a hot oiled pan.\n4. Add the noodles and sauce and toss until everything is coated and hot."],
  ["how do i make dumplings | homemade dumplings | potstickers recipe | gyoza recipe", "Potstickers:\n1. Mix 1/2 lb ground pork, 1 cup finely chopped cabbage, 1 green onion, 1 tsp ginger, 1 tbsp soy sauce and 1 tsp sesame oil.\n2. Put a teaspoon of filling in a round wrapper, wet the edge and pinch it closed.\n3. Fry the dumplings flat-side down in an oiled pan until golden, then add 1/3 cup water and cover.\n4. Steam 6–8 minutes until the water is gone and the filling is cooked. Dip in soy sauce and vinegar."],
  ["how do i make chickpea curry | chana masala recipe | vegan curry", "Chickpea curry (chana masala):\n1. Cook a chopped onion in oil until golden, then add garlic, ginger, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp coriander and 1 tsp garam masala.\n2. Add a can of crushed tomatoes and simmer 5 minutes.\n3. Stir in 2 cans of drained chickpeas and 1/2 cup water; simmer 15 minutes.\n4. Season with salt and lemon juice, top with cilantro, and serve with rice."],
  ["how do i make tomato basil pasta | quick pasta dinner | pasta with cherry tomatoes", "Quick cherry tomato pasta:\n1. Cook pasta in salted water.\n2. Meanwhile, cook 2 cups halved cherry tomatoes and 2 sliced garlic cloves in olive oil until the tomatoes burst and get saucy.\n3. Toss in the drained pasta with a splash of pasta water.\n4. Add torn basil, parmesan, salt and pepper."],
  ["how do i make chicken stir fry with peanut sauce | peanut sauce recipe | satay sauce", "Peanut sauce:\n1. Whisk 1/3 cup peanut butter, 2 tbsp soy sauce, 1 tbsp honey, 1 tbsp lime juice and 1 tsp grated garlic.\n2. Add warm water a spoonful at a time until smooth and pourable.\n3. Add chili sauce if you like heat.\n4. Toss with stir-fried chicken and veggies, or use as a dip for satay skewers and spring rolls. (Not for anyone with a peanut allergy!)"],
  ["how do i make jollof rice | jollof rice recipe", "Simple jollof rice:\n1. Blend 2 tomatoes, 1 red bell pepper, 1 onion and a little scotch bonnet pepper (optional) into a smooth sauce.\n2. Fry a chopped onion in oil, add 2 tbsp tomato paste and the blended sauce; cook 15 minutes until thick.\n3. Add thyme, curry powder, a bay leaf, salt and 2 cups rinsed long-grain rice, plus about 2 cups broth.\n4. Cover tightly and cook on low 25–30 minutes until the rice is tender."],
  ["how do i make fried plantains | plantain recipe | sweet plantains | maduros", "Fried sweet plantains:\n1. Use very ripe plantains (the skin should be mostly black).\n2. Peel and slice them diagonally about 1/2 inch thick.\n3. Fry in a little oil over medium heat 2–3 minutes per side until deep golden and caramelized.\n4. Drain on paper towels and sprinkle with salt. Have an adult handle the hot oil."],
  ["how do i make roasted potatoes | crispy roast potatoes | oven potatoes", "Crispy roast potatoes:\n1. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C).\n2. Cut potatoes into chunks and toss with olive oil, salt, pepper and garlic powder or rosemary.\n3. Spread in a single layer on a baking sheet.\n4. Roast 35–45 minutes, flipping halfway, until golden and crispy."],
  ["how do i make sweet potato fries | baked sweet potato fries", "Baked sweet potato fries:\n1. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C).\n2. Cut sweet potatoes into thin sticks and toss with 1 tbsp cornstarch, then oil and salt.\n3. Spread out on a lined baking sheet without crowding.\n4. Bake 25–30 minutes, flipping halfway, until crisp at the edges."],
  ["how do i make french fries | homemade fries | oven fries", "Homemade oven fries:\n1. Cut potatoes into 1/4-inch sticks and soak in cold water 30 minutes.\n2. Dry them very well with a towel.\n3. Toss with oil and salt and spread on a baking sheet.\n4. Bake at 450°F (230°C) for 25–35 minutes, flipping once, until golden."],
  ["how do i cook corn on the cob | boiled corn | how long to boil corn", "Corn on the cob:\n1. Remove the husks and silky threads.\n2. Bring a big pot of water to a boil.\n3. Add the corn and cook 4–6 minutes until bright yellow and tender.\n4. Use tongs to remove it (ask an adult), then add butter and salt."],
  ["how do i cook green beans | green beans recipe | garlic green beans", "Garlic green beans:\n1. Trim the ends off 1 lb green beans.\n2. Boil them 3–4 minutes until bright green, then drain.\n3. Melt butter or heat oil in a pan and cook 2 minced garlic cloves for 30 seconds.\n4. Toss in the beans, season with salt and pepper, and add a squeeze of lemon."],
  ["how do i make rice pilaf | rice pilaf recipe", "Rice pilaf:\n1. Melt 2 tbsp butter in a pot and cook a small chopped onion until soft.\n2. Add 1 cup long-grain rice (and a handful of broken spaghetti or orzo if you like) and stir until lightly toasted.\n3. Add 2 cups broth, bring to a boil, then cover and simmer on low 18 minutes.\n4. Let it sit covered 5 minutes, then fluff with a fork."],
  ["how do i cook couscous | couscous recipe | how to make couscous", "Couscous (the quick kind):\n1. Bring 1 cup water or broth to a boil with a pinch of salt and a little butter or oil.\n2. Stir in 1 cup couscous.\n3. Cover, turn off the heat and wait 5 minutes.\n4. Fluff with a fork. Add herbs, lemon or chopped veggies for extra flavor."],
  ["how do i cook quinoa | quinoa recipe | how to make quinoa", "Quinoa:\n1. Rinse 1 cup quinoa in a fine strainer (this removes a bitter coating).\n2. Put it in a pot with 2 cups water and a pinch of salt.\n3. Bring to a boil, cover, and simmer on low 15 minutes.\n4. Let it rest 5 minutes covered, then fluff with a fork."],
  ["how do i make glazed carrots | honey glazed carrots", "Honey-glazed carrots:\n1. Slice 1 lb carrots into coins.\n2. Simmer in a little salted water 5–7 minutes until just tender; drain.\n3. Add 2 tbsp butter and 1–2 tbsp honey or brown sugar to the pan.\n4. Toss over medium heat until glossy. A pinch of salt and some parsley finish it nicely."],
  ["how do i make cornbread | cornbread recipe", "Cornbread:\n1. Heat the oven to 400°F (200°C) and grease an 8-inch pan.\n2. Mix 1 cup cornmeal, 1 cup flour, 1/4 cup sugar, 1 tbsp baking powder and 1/2 tsp salt.\n3. Stir in 1 cup milk, 1 egg and 1/3 cup melted butter just until combined.\n4. Pour into the pan and bake 20–25 minutes until golden and a toothpick comes out clean."],
  ["how do i make mashed sweet potatoes | sweet potato mash", "Mashed sweet potatoes:\n1. Peel and cube 2 lb sweet potatoes.\n2. Boil 15 minutes until very soft; drain.\n3. Mash with 2 tbsp butter, a splash of milk and a pinch of salt.\n4. Add cinnamon and a drizzle of maple syrup for a sweet version."],
  ["how do i make stuffing | stuffing recipe | dressing recipe for thanksgiving", "Simple stuffing:\n1. Cut a loaf of bread into cubes and let it dry out overnight (or toast in the oven).\n2. Cook chopped onion and celery in 1/2 cup butter until soft; add sage, thyme, salt and pepper.\n3. Toss the bread with the veggies and about 2 cups warm broth plus 1 beaten egg.\n4. Bake in a greased dish at 350°F (175°C) for 35–45 minutes until golden on top."],
  ["how do i make mashed cauliflower | cauliflower mash", "Mashed cauliflower:\n1. Cut a head of cauliflower into florets.\n2. Steam or boil 10 minutes until very tender; drain well.\n3. Blend or mash with butter, a little garlic and a splash of milk.\n4. Season with salt and pepper. It's a lighter swap for mashed potatoes."],
  ["how do i make baked beans | baked beans recipe", "Quick baked beans:\n1. Cook a chopped onion (and some bacon if you like) in a pot until soft.\n2. Add 2 cans of drained navy or pinto beans.\n3. Stir in 1/2 cup ketchup, 2 tbsp brown sugar, 1 tbsp mustard and 1 tbsp molasses.\n4. Simmer 20 minutes, or bake at 350°F (175°C) for 45 minutes, until thick."],
  ["how do i make deviled eggs | deviled eggs recipe", "Deviled eggs:\n1. Hard-boil 6 eggs, cool, peel and cut in half lengthwise.\n2. Pop the yolks into a bowl and mash with 3 tbsp mayo, 1 tsp mustard, salt and pepper.\n3. Spoon or pipe the filling back into the whites.\n4. Sprinkle with paprika. Keep them chilled until serving."],
  ["how do i make ants on a log | ants on a log snack | celery with peanut butter", "Ants on a log:\n1. Wash celery sticks and cut them into 3–4 inch pieces.\n2. Fill the groove with peanut butter (or cream cheese or sunflower butter for nut-free).\n3. Press raisins along the top — those are the 'ants'.\n4. Try dried cranberries for 'ladybugs on a log'!"],
  ["how do i make salsa | homemade salsa | pico de gallo recipe | fresh salsa", "Fresh salsa (pico de gallo):\n1. Dice 4 tomatoes and 1/2 onion (ask an adult to help with the knife).\n2. Chop a handful of cilantro and, if you like heat, a little jalapeño (wash hands after!).\n3. Mix everything with the juice of 1 lime and a good pinch of salt.\n4. Let it sit 10 minutes and serve with chips or tacos."],
  ["how do i make kale chips | kale chips recipe", "Kale chips:\n1. Heat the oven to 300°F (150°C).\n2. Tear washed, very dry kale leaves off the stems into chip-size pieces.\n3. Toss with a little olive oil and salt and spread in one layer.\n4. Bake 15–20 minutes until crisp but not brown. Watch closely — they burn fast."],
  ["how do i make roasted chickpeas | crunchy chickpeas | chickpea snack", "Crunchy roasted chickpeas:\n1. Drain and rinse a can of chickpeas, then dry them very well.\n2. Toss with 1 tbsp oil, salt and spices (paprika, cumin or cinnamon sugar).\n3. Spread on a baking sheet.\n4. Roast at 400°F (200°C) for 25–35 minutes, shaking the pan now and then, until crunchy."],
  ["how do i make bruschetta | bruschetta recipe | tomato toast", "Bruschetta:\n1. Dice 3 ripe tomatoes and mix with chopped basil, a little garlic, olive oil and salt.\n2. Slice a baguette and toast or grill the slices.\n3. Rub each warm slice with a cut garlic clove (optional).\n4. Spoon the tomato mixture on top right before serving so the bread stays crisp."],
  ["how do i make energy balls | energy bites | no bake energy balls | protein balls", "No-bake energy balls:\n1. Mix 1 cup oats, 1/2 cup peanut butter (or sunflower butter), 1/3 cup honey and 1/2 tsp vanilla.\n2. Stir in 1/2 cup mini chocolate chips and 2 tbsp ground flax or chia (optional).\n3. Chill 20 minutes so it's easier to roll.\n4. Roll into 1-inch balls and keep them in the fridge for up to a week."],
  ["how do i make soft pretzels | homemade pretzels | soft pretzel recipe", "Soft pretzels:\n1. Mix 1 1/2 cups warm water, 1 packet yeast and 1 tbsp sugar; after 5 minutes add 4 cups flour and 1 tsp salt and knead 5 minutes.\n2. Let rise 1 hour, then divide into 8 ropes and twist into pretzel shapes.\n3. An adult should dip each one for about 30 seconds in simmering water with 1/3 cup baking soda.\n4. Sprinkle with coarse salt and bake at 425°F (220°C) for 12–15 minutes until deep brown."],
  ["how do i make tzatziki | tzatziki sauce recipe | cucumber yogurt dip", "Tzatziki (Greek cucumber-yogurt dip):\n1. Grate half a cucumber and squeeze out the water.\n2. Mix with 1 cup thick Greek yogurt.\n3. Add 1 small grated garlic clove, a squeeze of lemon, a little olive oil and chopped dill or mint.\n4. Season with salt and chill. Great with pita, gyros or veggies."],
  ["how do i make pigs in a blanket | pigs in a blanket recipe", "Pigs in a blanket:\n1. Heat the oven to 375°F (190°C).\n2. Cut a tube of crescent roll dough into thin triangles.\n3. Roll each cocktail sausage or hot dog piece up in a strip of dough.\n4. Bake 12–15 minutes until golden. Serve with ketchup or mustard."],
  ["how do i make spinach dip | spinach artichoke dip recipe", "Warm spinach-artichoke dip:\n1. Mix 8 oz softened cream cheese, 1/2 cup sour cream and 1/2 cup parmesan.\n2. Stir in a thawed, squeezed-dry box of frozen spinach and a chopped can of artichoke hearts.\n3. Add garlic, salt and 1 cup mozzarella.\n4. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 20–25 minutes until bubbly. Serve with chips or bread."],
  ["how do i make pita chips | homemade pita chips | baked pita chips", "Baked pita chips:\n1. Heat the oven to 375°F (190°C).\n2. Cut pita bread into triangles (split the layers apart for thinner chips).\n3. Brush or toss with olive oil and sprinkle with salt and spices.\n4. Bake 8–12 minutes until crisp and golden."],
  ["how do i make apple chips | baked apple chips", "Baked apple chips:\n1. Heat the oven to 225°F (110°C).\n2. Have an adult slice apples very thinly (a mandoline works best) and remove seeds.\n3. Lay the slices on parchment and sprinkle with cinnamon.\n4. Bake 1.5–2 hours, flipping once, until dry. They crisp up more as they cool."],
  ["how do i make cheese crisps | parmesan crisps | frico", "Parmesan crisps:\n1. Heat the oven to 400°F (200°C) and line a baking sheet with parchment.\n2. Make small mounds of grated parmesan (about 1 tbsp each), spaced apart.\n3. Bake 5–7 minutes until melted and golden.\n4. Let them cool on the pan — they turn crispy as they cool."],
  ["how do i make microwave popcorn in a paper bag | brown bag popcorn", "Paper-bag popcorn:\n1. Put 1/4 cup popcorn kernels in a plain brown paper lunch bag.\n2. Fold the top over twice.\n3. Microwave on high about 2–3 minutes, stopping when pops slow to 2 seconds apart.\n4. Open carefully (hot steam!) and add melted butter and salt. Never leave the microwave unattended."],
  ["how do i make a cheese board | charcuterie board ideas | snack board", "Snack board:\n1. Pick 2–3 cheeses (one soft, one hard, one fun).\n2. Add crackers or sliced baguette.\n3. Fill gaps with fruit (grapes, apple slices, berries), veggies, nuts and something salty like olives or pickles.\n4. Add a little dip or jam, and cured meats if you like."],
  ["how do i make frozen yogurt bark | yogurt bark recipe", "Frozen yogurt bark:\n1. Line a baking sheet with parchment.\n2. Spread 2 cups Greek yogurt (mixed with a little honey) about 1/4 inch thick.\n3. Sprinkle with berries, mini chocolate chips or granola.\n4. Freeze 2–3 hours until firm, then break into pieces."],
  ["how do i make mozzarella sticks | baked mozzarella sticks", "Baked mozzarella sticks:\n1. Cut string cheese in half and freeze 30 minutes.\n2. Dip in flour, then beaten egg, then Italian breadcrumbs (do the egg and crumbs twice for a thick coat).\n3. Freeze again 15 minutes so they don't melt too fast.\n4. Bake at 400°F (200°C) for 6–8 minutes until golden. Serve with marinara."],
  ["how do i make rice krispie treats | rice crispy treats recipe | marshmallow treats", "Rice crispy treats:\n1. Melt 3 tbsp butter in a big pot over low heat.\n2. Add a 10 oz bag of marshmallows and stir until melted.\n3. Remove from heat and stir in 6 cups crispy rice cereal.\n4. Press into a buttered 9x13 pan with a buttered spatula, cool, and cut into squares."],
  ["how do i make sugar cookies | sugar cookie recipe | cut out cookies", "Sugar cookies:\n1. Beat 1 cup softened butter with 1 cup sugar until fluffy; add 1 egg and 1 tsp vanilla.\n2. Mix in 2 3/4 cups flour, 1 tsp baking powder and 1/2 tsp salt.\n3. Chill the dough 1 hour, roll out 1/4 inch thick and cut shapes.\n4. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 8–10 minutes until the edges are just set. Cool before icing."],
  ["how do i make oatmeal cookies | oatmeal raisin cookies recipe", "Oatmeal raisin cookies:\n1. Beat 1/2 cup softened butter with 1/2 cup brown sugar and 1/4 cup white sugar; add 1 egg and 1 tsp vanilla.\n2. Mix in 3/4 cup flour, 1/2 tsp baking soda, 1 tsp cinnamon and a pinch of salt.\n3. Stir in 1 1/2 cups rolled oats and 3/4 cup raisins.\n4. Scoop onto a baking sheet and bake at 350°F (175°C) for 10–12 minutes."],
  ["how do i make peanut butter cookies | peanut butter cookie recipe | 3 ingredient cookies", "Three-ingredient peanut butter cookies:\n1. Mix 1 cup peanut butter, 1 cup sugar and 1 egg.\n2. Roll into 1-inch balls and place on a baking sheet.\n3. Press each with a fork to make a criss-cross pattern.\n4. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 10–12 minutes and let them cool on the pan (they're soft when hot)."],
  ["how do i make snickerdoodles | snickerdoodle recipe", "Snickerdoodles:\n1. Beat 1/2 cup butter with 3/4 cup sugar and 1 egg.\n2. Mix in 1 1/2 cups flour, 1 tsp cream of tartar, 1/2 tsp baking soda and a pinch of salt.\n3. Roll dough balls in a mix of 2 tbsp sugar and 2 tsp cinnamon.\n4. Bake at 375°F (190°C) for 8–10 minutes. Cream of tartar gives them their soft, tangy taste."],
  ["how do i make apple crisp | apple crisp recipe | apple crumble", "Apple crisp:\n1. Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Slice 6 apples and toss with 2 tbsp sugar, 1 tsp cinnamon and a squeeze of lemon; put in a baking dish.\n2. Mix 3/4 cup oats, 1/2 cup flour, 1/2 cup brown sugar and 1/2 cup cold butter until crumbly.\n3. Sprinkle the topping over the apples.\n4. Bake 40–45 minutes until bubbly and golden. Great with ice cream!"],
  ["how do i make apple pie | apple pie recipe", "Apple pie:\n1. Line a pie dish with one pie crust.\n2. Toss 6–7 sliced apples with 3/4 cup sugar, 2 tbsp flour, 1 tsp cinnamon and lemon juice; pile into the crust.\n3. Cover with a second crust, seal the edges and cut a few slits on top.\n4. Bake at 425°F (220°C) for 15 minutes, then 350°F (175°C) for 40–45 minutes until golden and bubbling."],
  ["how do i make pie crust | pie dough recipe | homemade pie crust", "Pie crust (one crust):\n1. Mix 1 1/4 cups flour and 1/2 tsp salt.\n2. Cut in 1/2 cup very cold cubed butter until pea-size bits remain.\n3. Sprinkle in 3–4 tbsp ice water, stirring, just until it holds together.\n4. Flatten into a disk, wrap and chill 1 hour before rolling. Cold butter = flaky crust!"],
  ["how do i make pumpkin pie | pumpkin pie recipe", "Pumpkin pie:\n1. Heat the oven to 425°F (220°C) and put an unbaked crust in a pie dish.\n2. Whisk a 15 oz can pumpkin puree, a 12 oz can evaporated milk, 2 eggs, 3/4 cup sugar, 1 1/2 tsp pumpkin pie spice and 1/2 tsp salt.\n3. Pour into the crust and bake 15 minutes.\n4. Lower to 350°F (175°C) and bake 40–50 minutes until the center barely jiggles. Cool completely."],
  ["how do i make no bake cheesecake | easy cheesecake recipe | cheesecake", "No-bake cheesecake:\n1. Mix 1 1/2 cups graham cracker crumbs with 6 tbsp melted butter and press into a pie dish.\n2. Beat 16 oz softened cream cheese with 1/2 cup sugar and 1 tsp vanilla.\n3. Fold in 1 cup whipped cream.\n4. Spread over the crust and chill at least 4 hours. Top with berries."],
  ["how do i make chocolate mousse | chocolate mousse recipe | easy mousse", "Easy chocolate mousse:\n1. Melt 4 oz chocolate and let it cool slightly.\n2. Whip 1 cup cold heavy cream with 1 tbsp sugar to soft peaks.\n3. Fold a spoonful of cream into the chocolate, then gently fold in the rest until no streaks remain.\n4. Spoon into cups and chill 2 hours."],
  ["how do i make pudding | chocolate pudding recipe | homemade pudding", "Homemade chocolate pudding:\n1. In a pot, whisk 1/2 cup sugar, 1/4 cup cocoa, 3 tbsp cornstarch and a pinch of salt.\n2. Slowly whisk in 2 1/2 cups milk.\n3. Cook over medium heat, whisking constantly, until it bubbles and thickens (about 5–8 minutes).\n4. Stir in 1 tsp vanilla and 1 tbsp butter, pour into cups and chill."],
  ["how do i make rice pudding | rice pudding recipe", "Rice pudding:\n1. Simmer 1/2 cup rice in 1 cup water until absorbed.\n2. Add 3 cups milk and 1/3 cup sugar and simmer gently, stirring often, 25–30 minutes until creamy.\n3. Stir in vanilla and a pinch of salt.\n4. Serve warm or cold with cinnamon or raisins."],
  ["how do i make s'mores | smores | s'mores in the oven | indoor smores", "S'mores:\n1. Break a graham cracker in half and put a piece of chocolate on one half.\n2. Toast a marshmallow over a campfire (with an adult!) until golden.\n3. Squish it between the crackers.\n4. Indoors: put the stacks on a baking sheet and broil 1–2 minutes, watching closely."],
  ["how do i make chocolate covered strawberries | chocolate dipped strawberries", "Chocolate-covered strawberries:\n1. Wash strawberries and dry them completely (water makes chocolate seize).\n2. Melt chocolate chips in the microwave in 30-second bursts, stirring each time.\n3. Hold each berry by the leaves and dip it in the chocolate.\n4. Place on parchment and chill 15–20 minutes until set."],
  ["how do i make fudge | easy fudge recipe | 2 ingredient fudge", "Easy chocolate fudge:\n1. Line an 8-inch pan with foil or parchment.\n2. Melt 3 cups chocolate chips with a 14 oz can sweetened condensed milk over low heat (or microwave in short bursts), stirring until smooth.\n3. Stir in 1 tsp vanilla and nuts if you like.\n4. Spread in the pan, chill 2 hours until firm and cut into squares."],
  ["how do i make lemon bars | lemon bars recipe", "Lemon bars:\n1. Mix 1 cup flour, 1/2 cup soft butter and 1/4 cup powdered sugar; press into an 8-inch pan and bake at 350°F (175°C) for 15–20 minutes.\n2. Whisk 2 eggs, 1 cup sugar, 2 tbsp flour and 1/3 cup lemon juice plus zest.\n3. Pour over the hot crust and bake 20 minutes until set.\n4. Cool completely, then dust with powdered sugar and cut."],
  ["how do i make cinnamon rolls | cinnamon roll recipe | cinnamon buns", "Cinnamon rolls:\n1. Make a sweet yeast dough (flour, milk, yeast, sugar, egg, butter, salt), knead and let rise until doubled.\n2. Roll into a rectangle, spread with soft butter and sprinkle with brown sugar and cinnamon.\n3. Roll up tightly, slice into 12 pieces and let rise again 30–45 minutes in a pan.\n4. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 22–25 minutes and drizzle with cream cheese icing."],
  ["how do i make a mug cake | microwave mug cake | cake in a mug", "Chocolate mug cake:\n1. In a big microwave-safe mug, mix 4 tbsp flour, 3 tbsp sugar, 2 tbsp cocoa and 1/4 tsp baking powder.\n2. Stir in 3 tbsp milk, 2 tbsp oil and a drop of vanilla until smooth.\n3. Microwave about 60–90 seconds until risen and just set.\n4. Let it cool a minute — the mug will be hot!"],
  ["how do i make ice cream in a bag | homemade ice cream without a machine | ice cream in a bag", "Ice cream in a bag:\n1. In a small zip bag, put 1/2 cup milk or half-and-half, 1 tbsp sugar and 1/4 tsp vanilla; seal well.\n2. Fill a large zip bag halfway with ice and add 1/3 cup salt.\n3. Put the small bag inside the big one and seal.\n4. Shake for 5–10 minutes (wear gloves — it gets very cold!) until it thickens. The salt makes the ice colder so the cream freezes."],
  ["how do i make nice cream | banana ice cream | one ingredient ice cream", "Banana 'nice cream':\n1. Peel ripe bananas, slice them and freeze for at least 4 hours.\n2. Blend the frozen slices in a food processor.\n3. Scrape down the sides — it looks crumbly at first, then turns creamy.\n4. Add cocoa, peanut butter or berries for flavor and eat right away."],
  ["how do i make churros | churro recipe | homemade churros", "Churros:\n1. Bring 1 cup water, 2 tbsp butter, 1 tbsp sugar and a pinch of salt to a boil; stir in 1 cup flour until a dough ball forms. Cool 5 minutes and beat in 1 egg.\n2. Pipe strips of dough through a star tip.\n3. An adult should fry them in 350°F (175°C) oil 2–3 minutes until golden.\n4. Roll in cinnamon sugar and dip in chocolate sauce."],
  ["how do i make caramel sauce | homemade caramel | salted caramel recipe", "Caramel sauce (adults only — melted sugar is extremely hot):\n1. Heat 1 cup sugar in a heavy pot over medium heat, stirring gently as it melts.\n2. Cook until it turns a deep amber color.\n3. Carefully add 6 tbsp butter (it will bubble up), then slowly pour in 1/2 cup heavy cream while whisking.\n4. Boil 1 minute, remove from heat and add a pinch of salt. It thickens as it cools."],
  ["how do i make chocolate bark | chocolate bark recipe | peppermint bark", "Chocolate bark:\n1. Line a baking sheet with parchment.\n2. Melt chocolate in 30-second microwave bursts, stirring each time.\n3. Spread it about 1/4 inch thick.\n4. Sprinkle with toppings (crushed candy canes, nuts, dried fruit, pretzels), chill until firm, and break into pieces."],
  ["how do i make shortbread | shortbread cookies recipe", "Scottish shortbread:\n1. Beat 1 cup softened butter with 1/2 cup sugar until creamy.\n2. Mix in 2 cups flour and a pinch of salt until a soft dough forms.\n3. Press into an 8-inch pan and prick all over with a fork.\n4. Bake at 325°F (160°C) for 30–35 minutes until pale golden. Cut while warm. Classic shortbread uses only butter, sugar and flour!"],
  ["how do i make blondies | blondie recipe | butterscotch brownies", "Blondies:\n1. Melt 1/2 cup butter and whisk with 1 cup brown sugar, 1 egg and 1 tsp vanilla.\n2. Stir in 1 cup flour, 1/2 tsp baking powder and a pinch of salt.\n3. Fold in 1/2 cup white or chocolate chips.\n4. Bake in a greased 8-inch pan at 350°F (175°C) for 20–25 minutes until golden."],
  ["how do i make jello | how to make jelly dessert | gelatin dessert", "Jello:\n1. Pour a box of gelatin powder into a bowl.\n2. Add 1 cup boiling water (ask an adult) and stir about 2 minutes until fully dissolved.\n3. Stir in 1 cup cold water.\n4. Refrigerate about 4 hours until firm. Note: fresh pineapple, kiwi and papaya stop it from setting!"],
  ["how do i make tiramisu | easy tiramisu | no bake tiramisu", "Easy tiramisu:\n1. Whisk 8 oz mascarpone with 1/2 cup sugar and 1 tsp vanilla, then fold in 1 cup whipped cream.\n2. Quickly dip ladyfinger biscuits in cooled coffee (or cocoa for a kid version).\n3. Layer biscuits, then cream, then repeat.\n4. Dust with cocoa and chill at least 4 hours. Tiramisu means 'pick me up' in Italian."],
  ["how do i make banana split | banana split recipe", "Banana split:\n1. Peel a banana and slice it in half lengthwise.\n2. Lay the halves in a long dish and add three scoops of ice cream (classically vanilla, chocolate and strawberry) in between.\n3. Drizzle with chocolate sauce, strawberry sauce and pineapple.\n4. Top with whipped cream, nuts and a cherry."],
  ["how do i make chocolate chip cookie bars | cookie bars recipe | pan cookie", "Chocolate chip cookie bars:\n1. Make your favorite chocolate chip cookie dough.\n2. Press it evenly into a greased 9x13 pan.\n3. Bake at 350°F (175°C) for 20–25 minutes until golden at the edges and just set in the middle.\n4. Cool completely before cutting into bars."],
  ["how do i make lemon cake | lemon drizzle cake recipe", "Lemon drizzle cake:\n1. Beat 1/2 cup butter and 3/4 cup sugar, then add 2 eggs, the zest of 1 lemon, 1 1/4 cups flour, 1 1/2 tsp baking powder and 1/3 cup milk.\n2. Bake in a lined loaf pan at 350°F (175°C) for 40–45 minutes.\n3. Mix the lemon's juice with 1/3 cup sugar.\n4. Poke holes in the warm cake and pour the drizzle over."],
  ["difference between crepes and pancakes | crepe vs pancake", "**Crepes** are thin and wide, made from runny batter with no baking powder. **Pancakes** (American style) are thick and fluffy because the batter contains baking powder, which makes bubbles as they cook."],
  ["how do i make a fruit pizza | fruit pizza recipe", "Fruit pizza:\n1. Bake a big sugar cookie crust on a pizza pan and let it cool.\n2. Beat 8 oz cream cheese with 1/3 cup powdered sugar and 1 tsp vanilla; spread over the cookie.\n3. Arrange sliced fruit (strawberries, kiwi, blueberries, mandarin oranges) on top.\n4. Chill and cut into slices."],
  ["how do i make baked apples | baked apple recipe", "Baked apples:\n1. Heat the oven to 375°F (190°C). Core 4 apples (adult job), leaving the bottom intact.\n2. Fill the centers with a mix of oats, brown sugar, cinnamon, raisins and a little butter.\n3. Stand them in a dish with 1/2 inch of water.\n4. Bake 35–45 minutes until tender."],
  ["how do i make chocolate truffles | truffle recipe | ganache", "Chocolate truffles:\n1. Heat 1/2 cup cream until steaming and pour over 8 oz chopped chocolate.\n2. Wait 2 minutes, then stir until smooth — this is **ganache**.\n3. Chill 1–2 hours until scoopable.\n4. Roll into balls and coat in cocoa, sprinkles or chopped nuts."],
  ["how do i make hot apple cider | warm cider | mulled apple cider", "Hot spiced apple cider:\n1. Pour apple cider or apple juice into a pot.\n2. Add cinnamon sticks, a few whole cloves and orange slices.\n3. Heat gently 15–20 minutes without boiling.\n4. Strain and serve in mugs. Your kitchen will smell amazing!"],
  ["how do i make chai | chai tea recipe | masala chai", "Masala chai:\n1. Simmer 1 cup water with a cinnamon stick, 3 crushed cardamom pods, 2 cloves and a slice of ginger for 5 minutes.\n2. Add 2 black tea bags (or 2 tsp loose tea) and simmer 2 minutes.\n3. Add 1 cup milk and sugar to taste; heat until steaming.\n4. Strain into cups. 'Chai' simply means tea in Hindi. It contains caffeine."],
  ["how do i make golden milk | turmeric latte | turmeric milk", "Golden milk:\n1. Warm 1 cup milk (any kind) in a small pot.\n2. Whisk in 1/2 tsp turmeric, a pinch of cinnamon, a pinch of ginger and a tiny pinch of black pepper.\n3. Sweeten with honey or maple syrup.\n4. Heat until steaming (don't boil) and pour into a mug."],
  ["how do i make fruit punch | punch recipe | party punch", "Party fruit punch:\n1. In a big bowl mix 4 cups fruit juice (like cranberry or pineapple) and 2 cups orange juice.\n2. Just before serving, add 4 cups sparkling water or lemon-lime soda.\n3. Float sliced oranges, lemons and berries on top.\n4. Add ice — or freeze juice in a ring mold so it doesn't water down."],
  ["how do i make a mango lassi | mango lassi recipe | lassi", "Mango lassi:\n1. Add 1 cup mango (fresh, frozen or canned pulp) to a blender.\n2. Add 1 cup plain yogurt and 1/2 cup milk.\n3. Add 1–2 tsp sugar or honey and a pinch of cardamom.\n4. Blend until smooth and serve cold. Lassi is a yogurt drink from India."],
  ["how do i make horchata | horchata recipe", "Horchata:\n1. Soak 1 cup uncooked white rice and a cinnamon stick in 4 cups water overnight.\n2. Blend everything until very smooth.\n3. Strain through a fine sieve or cloth.\n4. Stir in 1/2 cup sugar, a splash of vanilla and 1 cup milk (optional), then serve over ice. It's a classic Mexican drink."],
  ["how do i make agua fresca | agua fresca recipe | watermelon juice", "Watermelon agua fresca:\n1. Blend 4 cups chopped watermelon with 1 cup water.\n2. Strain if you want it smooth.\n3. Stir in the juice of 1 lime and a little sugar if needed.\n4. Serve over ice. 'Agua fresca' means 'fresh water' in Spanish."],
  ["how do i make infused water | fruit water | flavored water recipe", "Fruit-infused water:\n1. Fill a pitcher with cold water.\n2. Add sliced fruit and herbs — try cucumber + mint, strawberry + basil, or lemon + orange.\n3. Refrigerate 1–4 hours.\n4. Drink within a day or two. It's a tasty way to drink more water!"],
  ["how do i brew tea | how to make a cup of tea | how long to steep tea", "Making a cup of tea:\n1. Boil fresh water (ask an adult if you're young).\n2. Pour it over a tea bag or loose tea in a mug.\n3. Steep: black tea 3–5 minutes, green tea 2–3 minutes in slightly cooler water, herbal tea 5–7 minutes.\n4. Remove the tea and add milk, lemon or honey if you like."],
  ["how do i make bubble tea | boba tea recipe | how to make boba", "Bubble tea:\n1. Boil tapioca pearls according to the package (usually 5–15 minutes), then soak them in a brown sugar syrup.\n2. Brew strong black or green tea and let it cool.\n3. Put pearls in a glass, add ice, the tea and milk.\n4. Use a wide straw. Bubble tea was invented in Taiwan in the 1980s. (Pearls are a choking risk for little kids.)"],
  ["how do i make a slushie | slushie recipe | homemade slushy", "Homemade slushie:\n1. Freeze fruit juice in ice cube trays.\n2. Put the juice cubes in a blender with a splash of juice or water.\n3. Blend until slushy.\n4. Serve right away with a spoon or wide straw. Brain freeze warning!"],
  ["how do i make limeade | limeade recipe", "Limeade:\n1. Squeeze about 1 cup lime juice (around 8 limes).\n2. Make a simple syrup by dissolving 3/4 cup sugar in 1 cup hot water; let it cool.\n3. Mix juice, syrup and 4 cups cold water (or sparkling water).\n4. Serve over ice with lime slices."],
  ["how do i make a strawberry banana smoothie | strawberry banana smoothie", "Strawberry banana smoothie:\n1. Add 1 banana and 1 cup strawberries (frozen makes it thick) to a blender.\n2. Add 1/2 cup yogurt and 1/2 cup milk or juice.\n3. Blend until smooth.\n4. Taste and add honey if needed."],
  ["how do i make cold brew | cold brew coffee recipe", "Cold brew coffee:\n1. Mix 1 cup coarsely ground coffee with 4 cups cold water in a jar.\n2. Stir, cover and leave in the fridge 12–18 hours.\n3. Strain through a fine sieve and coffee filter.\n4. Mix the concentrate with water or milk over ice. Coffee has caffeine, so it's more of a grown-up drink."],
  ["is hot cocoa the same as hot chocolate | hot chocolate vs hot cocoa | difference between hot chocolate and hot cocoa", "**Hot cocoa** is made from cocoa powder, sugar and milk, so it's lighter. **Hot chocolate** is traditionally made by melting real chocolate (which includes cocoa butter) into milk, so it's richer and thicker. Many people use the names interchangeably."],
  ["how do i make eggnog | eggnog recipe | homemade eggnog", "Cooked eggnog (safer than raw egg versions):\n1. Whisk 4 egg yolks with 1/3 cup sugar.\n2. Heat 2 cups milk with a pinch of nutmeg and cinnamon until steaming.\n3. Slowly whisk the hot milk into the yolks, return to the pot and stir over low heat until it thickens slightly and reaches 160°F (71°C).\n4. Chill, stir in 1 cup cream and vanilla, and sprinkle nutmeg on top."],
  ["how do i make bread | homemade bread recipe | easy bread | how to bake bread", "Simple white bread:\n1. Mix 1 1/4 cups warm water, 2 1/4 tsp (1 packet) yeast and 1 tbsp sugar; wait 5–10 minutes until foamy.\n2. Add 3 1/2 cups flour, 1 1/2 tsp salt and 2 tbsp oil; knead 8–10 minutes until smooth and stretchy.\n3. Let rise covered until doubled (about 1 hour), shape into a loaf in a greased pan and rise 30–45 minutes more.\n4. Bake at 375°F (190°C) for 30–35 minutes until golden and hollow-sounding when tapped."],
  ["how do i make no knead bread | no-knead bread recipe | easy artisan bread", "No-knead bread:\n1. Stir 3 cups flour, 1 1/2 tsp salt, 1/4 tsp yeast and 1 1/2 cups warm water into a shaggy dough.\n2. Cover and leave at room temperature 12–18 hours.\n3. Shape into a ball on a floured surface and rest 1–2 hours.\n4. An adult should bake it in a preheated covered pot at 450°F (230°C) for 30 minutes, then uncovered 10–15 minutes until deep brown. Time does the kneading for you!"],
  ["how do i make pizza dough | pizza dough recipe | homemade pizza dough from scratch", "Pizza dough (2 medium pizzas):\n1. Mix 1 cup warm water, 2 1/4 tsp yeast and 1 tsp sugar; wait 5 minutes.\n2. Add 2 1/2–3 cups flour, 1 tsp salt and 2 tbsp olive oil; knead 5–8 minutes until smooth.\n3. Let rise in an oiled bowl, covered, about 1 hour until doubled.\n4. Punch down, divide, stretch into rounds, add toppings and bake at the hottest oven setting (450–500°F / 230–260°C) for 10–14 minutes."],
  ["how do i make flatbread | easy flatbread recipe | no yeast flatbread", "Two-ingredient yogurt flatbread:\n1. Mix 1 cup self-rising flour (or 1 cup flour + 1 1/2 tsp baking powder + pinch of salt) with 3/4 cup thick yogurt.\n2. Knead briefly into a soft dough and divide into 4.\n3. Roll each out thin.\n4. Cook in a hot dry pan 1–2 minutes per side until puffed with brown spots. Brush with garlic butter."],
  ["how do i make tortillas | homemade flour tortillas | tortilla recipe", "Flour tortillas:\n1. Mix 2 cups flour, 1 tsp salt and 1 tsp baking powder; rub in 3 tbsp oil or lard.\n2. Stir in about 3/4 cup warm water and knead into a soft dough; rest 20 minutes.\n3. Divide into 8 balls and roll each very thin.\n4. Cook in a hot dry pan 30–60 seconds per side until bubbly with light brown spots. Keep warm in a towel."],
  ["how do i make naan | naan bread recipe | homemade naan", "Easy naan:\n1. Mix 2 cups flour, 1 tsp baking powder, 1/2 tsp salt and 1 tsp sugar.\n2. Stir in 3/4 cup plain yogurt and 2 tbsp oil and knead into a soft dough; rest 30 minutes.\n3. Roll pieces into ovals.\n4. Cook in a very hot dry skillet 1–2 minutes per side until puffy and charred in spots, then brush with garlic butter."],
  ["how do i make focaccia | focaccia recipe", "Focaccia:\n1. Mix 4 cups flour, 2 tsp salt, 2 tsp yeast and 2 cups warm water into a sticky dough; let rise 1–2 hours.\n2. Pour 1/4 cup olive oil into a 9x13 pan and spread the dough to the edges.\n3. Rise 30 minutes, then poke dimples all over with oiled fingers and sprinkle with flaky salt and rosemary.\n4. Bake at 425°F (220°C) for 20–25 minutes until golden. It's a flat oven bread from Liguria, Italy."],
  ["how do i make dinner rolls | dinner roll recipe | soft rolls", "Soft dinner rolls:\n1. Mix 1 cup warm milk, 2 1/4 tsp yeast and 2 tbsp sugar; after 5 minutes add 1 egg, 1/4 cup melted butter, 1 tsp salt and about 3 1/2 cups flour.\n2. Knead until smooth and let rise until doubled (about 1 hour).\n3. Divide into 12 balls, place in a greased pan and let rise 30–45 minutes.\n4. Bake at 375°F (190°C) for 15–20 minutes and brush with butter."],
  ["how do i make a sourdough starter | sourdough starter | how to start sourdough", "Sourdough starter:\n1. Mix 1/2 cup whole wheat or all-purpose flour with 1/4 cup lukewarm water in a jar; cover loosely.\n2. Every 24 hours, throw out about half and 'feed' it with fresh flour and water in the same amounts.\n3. In 5–10 days it should double within several hours of feeding and smell pleasantly sour.\n4. Wild yeast and bacteria from the flour and air make it work — no packet yeast needed!"],
  ["why did my muffins sink | why did my cake sink in the middle | cake fell in the middle", "Cakes and muffins usually sink because they were **underbaked**, the oven door was opened too early, there was too much leavening (baking powder/soda), or the batter was overmixed. Check with a toothpick in the center — it should come out clean or with a few dry crumbs."],
  ["how do i know when cupcakes are done | is my cake done | toothpick test", "Insert a toothpick or skewer into the center of a cupcake or cake. If it comes out clean or with a few moist crumbs, it's done; if wet batter clings to it, bake a few minutes more. The top should also spring back when lightly pressed."],
  ["how do i make fudgy brownies | fudgy vs cakey brownies | why are my brownies cakey", "**Fudgy** brownies have more fat (butter and chocolate) and less flour; **cakey** brownies have more flour and often baking powder. For fudgy ones: use melted butter, don't overmix, and take them out when a toothpick has a few moist crumbs — they keep cooking as they cool."],
  ["why does my bread not rise | my dough didn't rise | yeast not working", "Bread usually doesn't rise because the yeast was **old or dead**, the water was too hot (above about 130°F / 54°C kills yeast), the room was too cold, or there was too much salt touching the yeast. Test yeast by mixing it with warm water and sugar — it should foam within 10 minutes."],
  ["what does creaming butter and sugar mean | how to cream butter and sugar | creaming method", "**Creaming** means beating softened butter and sugar together until pale and fluffy (about 2–4 minutes with a mixer). The sugar crystals cut tiny air pockets into the butter, which helps cakes and cookies rise and makes them tender."],
  ["why do recipes say room temperature butter | how to soften butter quickly | softening butter", "Room-temperature butter traps air better when creamed and mixes evenly, making lighter baked goods. To soften it fast, cut it into small cubes and leave it out 15–20 minutes, or grate cold butter. Microwaving can melt it, which changes the texture."],
  ["why do you sift flour | what does sift mean | do i need to sift flour", "**Sifting** pushes flour through a fine mesh to break up lumps and add air. It's helpful for delicate cakes and for mixing dry ingredients evenly. Many modern recipes don't require it — whisking dry ingredients together works well too."],
  ["what does gluten do in bread", "**Gluten** is a stretchy protein network that forms when wheat flour (also barley and rye) is mixed with water and kneaded. It traps gas bubbles so bread can rise and become chewy. People with celiac disease must avoid gluten."],
  ["why do you rest dough | why let pie dough rest | why chill cookie dough", "Resting dough lets the gluten relax, so it's easier to roll and doesn't shrink. Chilling cookie dough firms the butter so cookies spread less and lets the flour absorb liquid for deeper flavor and chewier texture."],
  ["why use parchment paper | parchment vs wax paper | can i bake with wax paper", "**Parchment paper** is coated with silicone and is heat-safe, so it's great for lining baking sheets. **Wax paper** has a wax coating that can melt and smoke in the oven, so use it only for no-heat jobs like wrapping or rolling dough."],
  ["what is self rising flour | how to make self rising flour", "**Self-rising flour** is flour with baking powder and salt already added. To make 1 cup, mix 1 cup all-purpose flour with 1 1/2 tsp baking powder and 1/4 tsp salt."],
  ["what is the difference between bread flour and all purpose flour | bread flour vs all purpose | cake flour vs all purpose", "Flours differ mainly in **protein**. Bread flour has the most (about 12–14%), making chewy bread; all-purpose is in the middle (about 10–12%) for most baking; cake flour has the least (about 7–9%) for very soft, tender cakes."],
  ["what is sushi | sushi | tell me about sushi | is sushi raw fish", "**Sushi** is a Japanese dish built around vinegared rice, often paired with raw or cooked fish, seafood, vegetables or egg and sometimes wrapped in seaweed (nori). Sushi means the seasoned rice — not raw fish. Raw fish served on its own is called **sashimi**."],
  ["what is sashimi | sashimi | sushi vs sashimi", "**Sashimi** is a Japanese dish of very fresh raw fish or seafood sliced thinly and served without rice, usually with soy sauce, wasabi and pickled ginger. Sushi always includes vinegared rice; sashimi doesn't."],
  ["what is a taco | what are tacos | where do tacos come from", "A **taco** is a Mexican dish: a small corn or flour tortilla folded around a filling such as grilled meat, fish, beans or vegetables, topped with things like onion, cilantro, salsa and lime. Tacos have been eaten in Mexico for centuries, long before crunchy hard-shell versions became popular in the US."],
  ["what is paella | paella | tell me about paella", "**Paella** is a rice dish from Valencia, Spain, cooked in a wide shallow pan with saffron, vegetables and often chicken, rabbit or seafood. The crispy, toasted rice layer at the bottom is called **socarrat** and is a favorite part."],
  ["what is pho | pho | how do you pronounce pho", "**Pho** (said like 'fuh') is a Vietnamese noodle soup with a clear, spiced broth (star anise, cinnamon, charred onion and ginger), flat rice noodles and thin slices of beef or chicken. It's topped at the table with herbs, bean sprouts, lime and chili."],
  ["what is ramen | ramen | tell me about ramen", "**Ramen** is a Japanese noodle soup with wheat noodles in a rich broth, commonly flavored with soy sauce (shoyu), miso, salt (shio) or pork bone (tonkotsu). Toppings include sliced pork, a soft-boiled egg, green onions, seaweed and bamboo shoots. Instant ramen was invented in Japan in 1958."],
  ["what is curry | curry | what does curry mean", "**Curry** is a broad name for dishes with a spiced sauce or gravy, found across South Asia, Southeast Asia, the Caribbean, Japan and beyond. Indian curries often use spices like cumin, coriander, turmeric and chili; Thai curries use curry pastes and coconut milk; Japanese curry is thick, mild and sweet."],
  ["what is kimchi | kimchi | tell me about kimchi", "**Kimchi** is Korea's famous fermented side dish, most often made from napa cabbage seasoned with chili, garlic, ginger and green onion. It's left to ferment so it becomes tangy and fizzy, and it's served with almost every Korean meal."],
  ["what is a croissant | croissant | where do croissants come from", "A **croissant** is a flaky, buttery, crescent-shaped pastry made by folding butter into yeast dough many times to create thin layers (called laminating). It's strongly linked to France, but it was inspired by the Austrian **kipferl**."],
  ["what is baklava | baklava | tell me about baklava", "**Baklava** is a sweet pastry made of many layers of paper-thin phyllo dough filled with chopped nuts (like walnuts or pistachios) and soaked in syrup or honey. It's popular in Turkey, Greece, the Middle East and the Balkans."],
  ["what is hummus | hummus | what is hummus made of", "**Hummus** is a creamy Middle Eastern dip made from cooked chickpeas blended with tahini (sesame paste), lemon juice, garlic and olive oil. It's eaten with pita bread or vegetables. 'Hummus' is the Arabic word for chickpea."],
  ["what is falafel | falafel | what is falafel made of", "**Falafel** are crispy fried balls or patties made from ground chickpeas or fava beans mixed with herbs and spices. They're a popular street food across the Middle East, often stuffed into pita with salad and tahini sauce."],
  ["what are pierogi | pierogi | what is a pierogi", "**Pierogi** are Polish dumplings made from unleavened dough folded around fillings like potato and cheese, sauerkraut and mushroom, meat, or sweet fruit. They're boiled and often then pan-fried in butter with onions."],
  ["what is poutine | poutine | tell me about poutine", "**Poutine** is a Canadian dish from Quebec: french fries topped with squeaky cheese curds and hot brown gravy. It became popular in the 1950s and is now loved all over Canada."],
  ["what is haggis | haggis | what is haggis made of", "**Haggis** is Scotland's national dish: a savory pudding of sheep's heart, liver and lungs minced with oats, onion, suet and spices, traditionally cooked inside a sheep's stomach. It's served with 'neeps and tatties' (turnips and potatoes), especially on Burns Night."],
  ["what is injera | injera | ethiopian bread", "**Injera** is a spongy, slightly sour flatbread from Ethiopia and Eritrea, made from a tiny grain called **teff** that's fermented before cooking. Stews are served right on top of it, and you tear off pieces of injera to scoop up your food — no forks needed!"],
  ["what is jollof rice | jollof | tell me about jollof rice", "**Jollof rice** is a beloved West African dish of rice cooked in a spicy tomato, pepper and onion sauce. It's eaten at parties across Nigeria, Ghana, Senegal and more, and countries playfully argue about who makes it best (the 'jollof wars')."],
  ["what is dim sum | dim sum | tell me about dim sum", "**Dim sum** is a Cantonese style of eating many small dishes — like steamed dumplings (har gow, siu mai), buns, rice rolls and egg tarts — usually with tea. It's traditionally served in the morning or at lunch, often from rolling carts. The practice is called **yum cha** ('drink tea')."],
  ["what is pad thai | pad thai | tell me about pad thai", "**Pad thai** is a Thai stir-fried rice noodle dish with egg, tofu or shrimp, bean sprouts and green onions in a sweet-sour sauce of tamarind, fish sauce and palm sugar. It's topped with crushed peanuts and lime. It became a national dish of Thailand in the mid-1900s."],
  ["what is biryani | biryani | tell me about biryani", "**Biryani** is a fragrant South Asian rice dish where long-grain basmati rice is layered with spiced meat (or vegetables), saffron, fried onions and herbs, then cooked slowly together. There are many regional styles, like Hyderabadi and Lucknowi biryani."],
  ["what is goulash | goulash | hungarian goulash", "**Goulash** (gulyás) is a Hungarian stew or soup of beef, onions and potatoes, flavored with plenty of **paprika**. It was originally cooked by Hungarian cattle herders in kettles over open fires."],
  ["what is borscht | borscht | what is borscht made of", "**Borscht** is a sour soup popular in Ukraine and across Eastern Europe, best known in its bright red form made with **beets**, cabbage, potatoes and often meat. It's usually served with a dollop of sour cream and dill."],
  ["what is fondue | fondue | cheese fondue", "**Fondue** is a Swiss dish of melted cheese (often Gruyère and Emmental with wine or another liquid) kept warm in a shared pot. People dip cubes of bread into it using long forks. There are also chocolate fondues for dessert."],
  ["what is gelato | gelato | gelato vs ice cream | difference between gelato and ice cream", "**Gelato** is Italian-style ice cream. Compared with American ice cream, it usually has more milk and less cream, little or no egg yolk, is churned more slowly (so less air), and is served a bit warmer — making it dense and intensely flavored."],
  ["what are churros | churros | where are churros from", "**Churros** are fried dough pastries, piped through a star-shaped tip so they're ridged, then rolled in sugar (often with cinnamon). They're popular in Spain and Latin America, where they're often dunked in thick hot chocolate."],
  ["what is mochi | mochi | what is mochi made of", "**Mochi** is a soft, chewy Japanese rice cake made from glutinous (sticky) rice pounded or made into a dough. It can be filled with sweet red bean paste (daifuku) or wrapped around ice cream. Mochi is especially popular at New Year. Small bites are best — it's sticky!"],
  ["what is a burrito | burrito | where do burritos come from", "A **burrito** is a large flour tortilla wrapped around fillings like beans, rice, meat, cheese and salsa. It comes from northern Mexico, and the big 'Mission-style' burrito was popularized in San Francisco. 'Burrito' means 'little donkey' in Spanish."],
  ["what is a quesadilla vs a taco | difference between taco and burrito | taco vs burrito", "A **taco** is a small tortilla folded around a filling and eaten open. A **burrito** is a big flour tortilla fully wrapped around its filling. A **quesadilla** is a tortilla filled mainly with cheese, folded and toasted until melty."],
  ["what is pizza | where did pizza come from | who invented pizza", "Modern **pizza** comes from **Naples, Italy**, where flatbreads topped with tomato became popular street food in the 1700s–1800s. The Margherita pizza (tomato, mozzarella and basil) is famously linked to Queen Margherita in 1889. Italian immigrants brought pizza to the United States in the early 1900s."],
  ["what is pasta | where does pasta come from | who invented pasta", "**Pasta** is a dough of wheat flour (usually durum wheat semolina) and water or eggs, shaped and cooked. It's strongly linked to Italy, where dried pasta was being made by the 1100s in Sicily. The popular story that Marco Polo brought pasta from China is a myth — noodles developed independently in China and Italy."],
  ["what is a samosa | samosa | samosas", "A **samosa** is a fried or baked triangular pastry filled with spiced potatoes, peas, onions and sometimes meat or lentils. It's a hugely popular snack in India, Pakistan and across South Asia, the Middle East and East Africa, often served with chutney."],
  ["what is a dumpling | dumplings | types of dumplings", "A **dumpling** is a piece of dough, either filled or plain, that's boiled, steamed or fried. Nearly every culture has one: Chinese jiaozi, Japanese gyoza, Polish pierogi, Nepali momo, Italian ravioli, Russian pelmeni and Korean mandu are all dumplings!"],
  ["what are tamales | tamale | tamales", "**Tamales** are a Mesoamerican dish of masa (corn dough) filled with meat, cheese, chilies or sweet fillings, wrapped in a corn husk or banana leaf and steamed. They've been made for thousands of years and are a holiday tradition in Mexico and Central America. Don't eat the husk!"],
  ["what is ceviche | ceviche", "**Ceviche** is a dish of raw fish or seafood 'cooked' by marinating in citrus juice (usually lime) with onion, chili and cilantro. The acid changes the proteins so the fish turns firm and opaque. It's Peru's national dish and popular across Latin America."],
  ["what is a crepe | crepe | what are crepes", "A **crepe** is a very thin French pancake. Sweet crepes are filled with things like Nutella, fruit or sugar and lemon; savory buckwheat versions from Brittany are called **galettes** and often hold ham, cheese and egg."],
  ["what is ratatouille | ratatouille", "**Ratatouille** is a vegetable stew from Provence in southern France, made with tomatoes, eggplant, zucchini, bell peppers, onions, garlic and herbs like thyme and basil, slowly cooked in olive oil."],
  ["what is a baguette | baguette", "A **baguette** is a long, thin French bread with a crisp golden crust and a chewy, airy inside. Traditional French baguettes are made from just flour, water, salt and yeast. In 2022, UNESCO recognized the craft of French baguette baking as cultural heritage."],
  ["what is a bagel | bagel | why are bagels chewy", "A **bagel** is a ring-shaped bread that's briefly **boiled** before baking, which gives it a shiny crust and dense, chewy inside. Bagels originated in Jewish communities in Poland and became famous in New York City."],
  ["what is tofu | tofu | what is tofu made of", "**Tofu** is made by curdling soy milk and pressing the curds into blocks — a bit like how cheese is made from milk. It originated in China about 2,000 years ago. It's high in protein and soaks up flavors from sauces and marinades."],
  ["what is tempura | tempura", "**Tempura** is a Japanese dish of seafood or vegetables dipped in a light, cold batter and deep-fried until crisp and pale. The technique was influenced by Portuguese visitors to Japan in the 1500s."],
  ["what is teriyaki | teriyaki | what does teriyaki mean", "**Teriyaki** is a Japanese cooking style where food is grilled or broiled and glazed with a sauce of soy sauce, mirin (sweet rice wine) and sugar. 'Teri' means shine and 'yaki' means grilled."],
  ["what is miso | miso | miso soup", "**Miso** is a salty, savory Japanese paste made from fermented soybeans (often with rice or barley). It's used in **miso soup** — a broth called dashi with miso, tofu, seaweed and green onions — as well as marinades and dressings."],
  ["what is bibimbap | bibimbap", "**Bibimbap** is a Korean dish whose name means 'mixed rice'. A bowl of warm rice is topped with seasoned vegetables, meat, a fried egg and spicy gochujang chili paste, then mixed together before eating. A version served in a sizzling stone bowl is called dolsot bibimbap."],
  ["what is bulgogi | bulgogi | korean bbq", "**Bulgogi** ('fire meat') is a Korean dish of thinly sliced beef marinated in soy sauce, sugar, sesame oil, garlic and pear, then grilled or pan-cooked. It's often wrapped in lettuce leaves with rice and sauce."],
  ["what is a gyro | gyro | gyro vs shawarma | what is shawarma", "**Gyros** (Greek) and **shawarma** (Middle Eastern) are both made from seasoned meat stacked on a vertical rotating spit, shaved off in thin slices and wrapped in pita. Gyros are usually served with tzatziki; shawarma often with garlic sauce or tahini. Turkish döner kebab is their common ancestor."],
  ["what is a kebab | kebab | kabob", "A **kebab** is grilled or roasted meat, originally from the Middle East and Central Asia. **Shish kebab** means chunks of meat on a skewer; **döner kebab** is meat cooked on a vertical spit; **kofta** kebabs are spiced ground meat shaped around a skewer."],
  ["what is couscous | couscous | is couscous a grain", "**Couscous** is tiny steamed balls of durum wheat semolina — so it's actually a type of pasta, not a grain. It's a staple in North Africa (Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia), traditionally steamed over a stew in a pot called a couscoussier."],
  ["what is tagine | tagine | tajine", "A **tagine** is a slow-cooked North African stew of meat or vegetables with spices, often sweetened with dried fruit like apricots or prunes. It's named after the clay pot it's cooked in, which has a cone-shaped lid that returns moisture to the food."],
  ["what is gazpacho | gazpacho | cold soup", "**Gazpacho** is a cold Spanish soup from Andalusia, made by blending raw tomatoes, cucumber, bell pepper, garlic, olive oil, vinegar and sometimes bread. It's a refreshing summer dish."],
  ["what are tapas | tapas", "**Tapas** are small plates or snacks from Spain — like olives, patatas bravas, Spanish tortilla, croquettes and garlic shrimp — usually shared with friends. One story says they began as a slice of bread placed over a drink to keep flies out ('tapar' means to cover)."],
  ["what is a spanish tortilla | tortilla espanola | spanish omelette", "A **Spanish tortilla** (tortilla de patatas) is a thick omelette of eggs and potatoes slowly cooked in olive oil, often with onion. It's served in slices, warm or at room temperature. It has nothing to do with the Mexican flatbread of the same name!"],
  ["what is risotto | risotto", "**Risotto** is a creamy northern Italian rice dish made with short-grain rice (like arborio or carnaroli) slowly cooked in broth while stirring. The starch released from the rice creates the creaminess — no cream needed."],
  ["what is lasagna | lasagna | where is lasagna from", "**Lasagna** is an Italian baked dish of wide, flat pasta sheets layered with sauce and cheese. The classic version from Bologna (lasagne alla bolognese) uses meat ragù and creamy béchamel sauce."],
  ["what is gnocchi | gnocchi", "**Gnocchi** are small Italian dumplings, most often made from potato, flour and sometimes egg. They're boiled until they float, then served with butter and sage, tomato sauce or pesto."],
  ["what is ravioli | ravioli", "**Ravioli** are Italian pasta pillows: two thin sheets of pasta sealed around a filling such as ricotta and spinach, meat or pumpkin, then boiled and served with sauce."],
  ["what is pesto | pesto", "**Pesto** is an uncooked sauce from Genoa, Italy, made by crushing fresh basil, pine nuts, garlic, parmesan or pecorino and olive oil. The name comes from 'pestare', to pound, because it was traditionally made with a mortar and pestle."],
  ["what is bruschetta | bruschetta | how do you pronounce bruschetta", "**Bruschetta** (said 'broo-SKET-ta' in Italian) is toasted bread rubbed with garlic and drizzled with olive oil, often topped with chopped tomatoes and basil. It's a classic Italian appetizer."],
  ["what is tiramisu | tiramisu", "**Tiramisu** is an Italian dessert of coffee-soaked ladyfinger biscuits layered with a whipped mascarpone cream and dusted with cocoa. The name means 'pick me up', probably because of the coffee."],
  ["what is cannoli | cannoli", "**Cannoli** are Sicilian pastries: crispy fried tubes of dough filled with sweet, creamy ricotta, often with chocolate chips or candied fruit."],
  ["what is a pretzel | pretzel | where do pretzels come from", "A **pretzel** is a baked bread twisted into a knot shape. Soft pretzels are dipped in an alkaline (baking soda or lye) bath before baking, giving them a deep brown, shiny crust. Pretzels are closely tied to southern Germany, where they're called Brezeln."],
  ["what is schnitzel | schnitzel | wiener schnitzel", "**Schnitzel** is a thin slice of meat that's pounded flat, breaded and fried. **Wiener schnitzel** is the famous Austrian version made with veal and served with lemon and potato salad."],
  ["what is sauerkraut | sauerkraut", "**Sauerkraut** ('sour cabbage' in German) is finely shredded cabbage fermented with salt. Lactic acid bacteria turn it tangy. It's popular in Germany and Central/Eastern Europe, and it's a cousin of Korean kimchi."],
  ["what is bratwurst | bratwurst", "**Bratwurst** is a German sausage usually made from pork (sometimes veal or beef) seasoned with spices like nutmeg, marjoram and caraway. It's often grilled and served in a roll with mustard."],
  ["what is a crumpet | crumpet | crumpet vs english muffin", "A **crumpet** is a British griddle cake made from a runny yeast batter, cooked in a ring on one side so the top fills with little holes. Those holes soak up butter! An English muffin is made from a firmer dough and is split before toasting."],
  ["what is fish and chips | fish and chips", "**Fish and chips** is a classic British meal of battered, deep-fried white fish (usually cod or haddock) served with thick-cut fried potatoes ('chips'), often with salt, vinegar and mushy peas. It became popular in England in the 1860s."],
  ["what is a scone | scone", "A **scone** is a lightly sweet British baked good, similar to an American biscuit, made with flour, butter, milk and baking powder. It's traditionally served with clotted cream and jam as part of afternoon tea."],
  ["what is a pasty | cornish pasty", "A **Cornish pasty** is a half-moon pastry from Cornwall, England, filled with beef, potato, swede (rutabaga) and onion. Tin miners carried them for lunch, and the thick crimped edge could be held with dirty hands."],
  ["what is a pavlova | pavlova", "**Pavlova** is a meringue dessert with a crisp shell and soft, marshmallowy center, topped with whipped cream and fresh fruit. It was named after the Russian ballerina Anna Pavlova, and both Australia and New Zealand claim to have invented it."],
  ["what is vegemite | vegemite | what is marmite", "**Vegemite** (Australia) and **Marmite** (UK) are dark, salty spreads made from leftover brewer's yeast extract. They taste very strong and savory — people usually spread them thinly on buttered toast. You either love them or hate them!"],
  ["what is a lamington | lamington", "A **lamington** is an Australian cake: a square of sponge cake dipped in chocolate icing and rolled in desiccated coconut. Some have a layer of jam or cream in the middle."],
  ["what is a tortilla | tortilla | corn vs flour tortilla", "A **tortilla** is a thin, round flatbread from Mexico and Central America. Corn tortillas are made from **masa** (corn treated with lime and ground); flour tortillas are made from wheat flour, fat and water and are more common in northern Mexico."],
  ["what is masa | masa harina | nixtamalization", "**Masa** is corn dough made by cooking dried corn in an alkaline solution (usually with lime, calcium hydroxide), then grinding it. This ancient Mesoamerican process, called **nixtamalization**, makes the corn more nutritious and lets it form a dough for tortillas and tamales."],
  ["what is mole | mole sauce", "**Mole** (said 'MO-lay') is a family of rich Mexican sauces. Mole poblano from Puebla can contain over 20 ingredients, including several dried chilies, spices, nuts, seeds and a bit of chocolate. It's often served over chicken or turkey."],
  ["what is an empanada | empanada | empanadas", "An **empanada** is a folded pastry turnover filled with savory ingredients like seasoned beef, chicken, cheese or vegetables, then baked or fried. They're popular across Spain, Latin America and the Philippines, and each country has its own style."],
  ["what is an arepa | arepa", "An **arepa** is a round, thick cornmeal cake from Venezuela and Colombia. It's grilled or baked, then split and stuffed with fillings like cheese, shredded beef, black beans or avocado."],
  ["what are pupusas | pupusa", "A **pupusa** is El Salvador's national dish: a thick corn tortilla stuffed with cheese, beans or pork, cooked on a griddle and served with curtido (tangy cabbage slaw) and tomato salsa."],
  ["what is feijoada | feijoada", "**Feijoada** is Brazil's national dish: a hearty stew of black beans with pork and beef, served with rice, collard greens, orange slices and farofa (toasted cassava flour)."],
  ["what is pao de queijo | brazilian cheese bread", "**Pão de queijo** is Brazilian cheese bread: small, chewy-puffy rolls made from tapioca (cassava) starch, eggs, milk and cheese. Because they use tapioca, they're naturally gluten-free."],
  ["what is jerk chicken | jerk chicken | jamaican jerk", "**Jerk** is a Jamaican way of seasoning and cooking meat, especially chicken or pork, with a fiery marinade of scotch bonnet peppers and allspice (called pimento in Jamaica), plus thyme, garlic and green onion. It's traditionally smoked over pimento wood."],
  ["what is gumbo | gumbo", "**Gumbo** is a thick stew from Louisiana made with a dark roux (cooked flour and fat), the 'holy trinity' of onion, celery and bell pepper, plus meat or seafood, and often okra or filé powder. It's served over rice and mixes West African, French, Spanish and Native American influences."],
  ["what is cajun food | cajun vs creole", "**Cajun** food comes from the French-Canadian Acadians who settled in rural Louisiana — rustic, hearty and well-spiced. **Creole** food developed in New Orleans with French, Spanish, African and Caribbean influences and often includes tomatoes. Both use the 'holy trinity' of onion, celery and bell pepper."],
  ["what are pelmeni | pelmeni", "**Pelmeni** are small Russian dumplings filled with minced meat, wrapped in thin unleavened dough and boiled. They're usually served with sour cream or butter. They're smaller and meatier than Polish pierogi."],
  ["what is a blini | blini", "**Blini** are thin Russian and Ukrainian pancakes, sometimes made with buckwheat and yeast. They're eaten with sour cream, jam, or caviar, and they're especially popular during Maslenitsa, a festival that says goodbye to winter."],
  ["what is crepes suzette | crepe suzette", "**Crêpes Suzette** is a French dessert of crepes in a warm sauce of caramelized sugar, butter and orange juice and zest, traditionally flambéed with orange liqueur at the table."],
  ["what is quiche | quiche", "A **quiche** is a French savory tart with a pastry crust filled with eggs, cream or milk, cheese and other ingredients. The best-known version, quiche Lorraine, includes bacon."],
  ["what is a souffle | souffle | why do souffles fall", "A **soufflé** is a light, puffy French baked dish made with a flavored base and beaten egg whites. The air bubbles in the egg whites expand in the oven, making it rise. As it cools, the air contracts, which is why soufflés fall soon after baking."],
  ["what is a macaron | macaron | macaron vs macaroon", "A **macaron** is a French sandwich cookie made from almond flour, sugar and egg whites, with smooth tops, ruffled 'feet' and a filling of ganache or buttercream. A **macaroon** is a chewy mound made mostly from coconut."],
  ["what is creme brulee | creme brulee", "**Crème brûlée** ('burnt cream') is a rich vanilla custard topped with a thin layer of sugar that's caramelized into a hard, glassy crust using a kitchen torch or broiler. You crack the top with a spoon."],
  ["what is an eclair | eclair | eclairs", "An **éclair** is a French pastry: a long shell of choux pastry filled with pastry cream and topped with chocolate glaze. Choux dough puffs up from steam, leaving a hollow inside for the filling."],
  ["what is a waffle | belgian waffle vs regular waffle", "A **waffle** is a batter or dough cooked between two patterned hot plates, giving it its grid of pockets. **Belgian waffles** have deeper pockets and are often lighter and crispier, sometimes made with yeast; the **Liège waffle** is a dense, sweet one with pearl sugar."],
  ["what is a stroopwafel | stroopwafel", "A **stroopwafel** is a Dutch treat of two thin, crisp waffle cookies stuck together with a layer of caramel syrup. A popular trick is to rest it on top of a hot drink so the caramel softens."],
  ["what is smorgasbord | smorgasbord", "A **smörgåsbord** is a Swedish buffet-style meal with many dishes like pickled herring, meatballs, cheeses, cured salmon and breads. In English, the word now means any big variety of things."],
  ["what is lutefisk | lutefisk", "**Lutefisk** is a traditional Nordic dish of dried whitefish (usually cod) soaked in water and lye, then rinsed and cooked. It has a jelly-like texture and is mainly eaten around Christmas in Norway and Sweden and by their descendants in the US."],
  ["what is a cinnamon bun in sweden | kanelbulle | swedish cinnamon buns", "A **kanelbulle** is a Swedish cinnamon bun, often flavored with cardamom and topped with pearl sugar instead of icing. Sweden even celebrates Cinnamon Bun Day (Kanelbullens dag) on October 4."],
  ["what is laksa | laksa", "**Laksa** is a spicy noodle soup from Malaysia, Singapore and Indonesia. Curry laksa has a rich coconut-curry broth, while Penang (asam) laksa has a sour tamarind-fish broth."],
  ["what is nasi goreng | nasi goreng", "**Nasi goreng** ('fried rice' in Indonesian and Malay) is fried rice flavored with sweet soy sauce (kecap manis), shallots, garlic and chili, usually topped with a fried egg. It's often called Indonesia's national dish."],
  ["what is satay | satay | sate", "**Satay** is skewered, marinated meat grilled over charcoal and served with a peanut sauce. It's a famous street food in Indonesia, Malaysia, Singapore and Thailand."],
  ["what is adobo | filipino adobo | chicken adobo", "**Adobo** is a Filipino dish of chicken or pork braised in vinegar, soy sauce, garlic, bay leaves and black peppercorns. It's tangy and savory, and many consider it the unofficial national dish of the Philippines."],
  ["what is tom yum | tom yum soup", "**Tom yum** is a hot and sour Thai soup flavored with lemongrass, galangal, kaffir lime leaves, lime juice, fish sauce and chilies, often with shrimp (tom yum goong)."],
  ["what is green curry | thai green curry", "**Thai green curry** is made with a paste of fresh green chilies, lemongrass, galangal, garlic and herbs, cooked with coconut milk, meat or tofu, and Thai eggplant, finished with Thai basil. It's often one of the spiciest Thai curries."],
  ["what is a spring roll | spring roll vs egg roll", "**Spring rolls** are thin wrappers rolled around vegetables (and sometimes meat), served fried or fresh. Vietnamese fresh rolls use rice paper and aren't fried. **Egg rolls**, an American Chinese creation, have a thicker, bubbly wheat wrapper and are always deep-fried."],
  ["what is banh mi | banh mi", "**Bánh mì** is a Vietnamese sandwich on a light, crispy baguette — a legacy of French colonial times — filled with meat (like grilled pork or pâté), pickled carrots and daikon, cucumber, cilantro, chili and mayo."],
  ["what is peking duck | peking duck", "**Peking duck** is a famous dish from Beijing: duck roasted until the skin is thin and crispy, then sliced and served with thin pancakes, sweet bean or hoisin sauce, cucumber and green onion."],
  ["what is mapo tofu | mapo tofu", "**Mapo tofu** is a spicy dish from Sichuan, China, made with soft tofu in a chili-bean sauce with ground pork and Sichuan peppercorns, which create a tingly, numbing feeling on your tongue."],
  ["what is kung pao chicken | kung pao chicken", "**Kung pao chicken** is a Sichuan stir-fry of diced chicken, peanuts, dried chilies and Sichuan peppercorns in a savory-sweet-sour sauce. It's named after a Qing dynasty official whose title was 'Gongbao'."],
  ["what is a fortune cookie | where do fortune cookies come from | are fortune cookies chinese", "**Fortune cookies** are crisp folded cookies with a paper message inside. Despite being served in Chinese restaurants in the US, they're not from China — historians trace them to Japanese bakers in California in the early 1900s."],
  ["what is naan | naan", "**Naan** is a soft, puffy flatbread from South and Central Asia, traditionally baked by slapping it onto the hot inner wall of a clay oven called a **tandoor**. It's often brushed with butter or garlic and served with curries."],
  ["what is tandoori chicken | tandoori", "**Tandoori chicken** is chicken marinated in yogurt and spices (like chili, cumin and garam masala), then cooked in a very hot clay oven called a tandoor. Its red color often comes from Kashmiri chili."],
  ["what is paneer | paneer", "**Paneer** is a fresh, firm, mild cheese from South Asia made by curdling hot milk with lemon juice or vinegar and pressing the curds. It doesn't melt when heated, so it's used in dishes like palak paneer (spinach) and paneer tikka."],
  ["what is a dosa | dosa", "A **dosa** is a thin, crispy South Indian crepe made from a fermented batter of rice and lentils. It's often filled with spiced potatoes (masala dosa) and served with sambar (lentil stew) and coconut chutney."],
  ["what is ghee | ghee", "**Ghee** is clarified butter that's been simmered until the milk solids brown slightly and are strained out, giving it a nutty flavor. It's widely used in Indian cooking and has a high smoke point."],
  ["what is chutney | chutney", "**Chutney** is a South Asian condiment that can be sweet, sour or spicy. It can be fresh (like mint-cilantro chutney) or cooked and jam-like (like mango chutney)."],
  ["what is shakshuka | shakshuka", "**Shakshuka** is a North African and Middle Eastern dish of eggs gently poached in a spiced sauce of tomatoes, peppers and onions. It's often eaten for breakfast with bread for dipping."],
  ["what is tabbouleh | tabbouleh", "**Tabbouleh** is a Levantine salad (from Lebanon and Syria) made mostly of finely chopped parsley with tomatoes, mint, onion and a little bulgur wheat, dressed with olive oil and lemon juice."],
  ["what is tahini | tahini", "**Tahini** is a paste made from ground toasted sesame seeds. It's a key ingredient in hummus and baba ghanoush and makes a creamy sauce when mixed with lemon, garlic and water."],
  ["what is baba ganoush | baba ghanoush", "**Baba ghanoush** is a smoky Middle Eastern dip made from roasted or charred eggplant mashed with tahini, lemon juice, garlic and olive oil."],
  ["what is pita bread | pita | why does pita have a pocket", "**Pita** is a round flatbread from the Middle East and Mediterranean. It's baked in a very hot oven, where steam quickly puffs the dough up like a balloon and separates the layers — that's what creates the pocket."],
  ["what is tortellini | tortellini", "**Tortellini** are small ring-shaped stuffed pasta from Emilia-Romagna, Italy, traditionally filled with meat and cheese and served in broth. Legend says their shape was inspired by Venus's belly button!"],
  ["what is a calzone | calzone", "A **calzone** is a folded pizza from Naples, Italy: pizza dough folded in half over fillings like mozzarella, ricotta and meats, sealed and baked (or fried)."],
  ["what is polenta | polenta", "**Polenta** is a northern Italian dish of cornmeal cooked slowly in water or broth until creamy. It can be served soft like porridge, or cooled, sliced and grilled or fried."],
  ["what is a chimichanga | chimichanga", "A **chimichanga** is a deep-fried burrito, popular in Tex-Mex and Sonoran-style cooking. Arizona restaurants claim to have invented it."],
  ["what is tex mex | tex-mex food", "**Tex-Mex** is a cooking style that blends Mexican and Texan traditions, featuring dishes like chili con carne, fajitas, nachos, hard-shell tacos and lots of yellow cheese and cumin."],
  ["what is barbecue | bbq | what is american barbecue", "In the US, **barbecue** means meat cooked slowly with low heat and smoke, like brisket, ribs and pulled pork. Regional styles differ: Texas focuses on beef brisket, the Carolinas on pork with vinegar or mustard sauces, Memphis on ribs, and Kansas City on sweet tomato-based sauces."],
  ["what is a hamburger | where did the hamburger come from | why is it called a hamburger", "A **hamburger** is a cooked ground beef patty served in a bun. It's named after Hamburg, Germany, where a seasoned minced-beef 'Hamburg steak' was popular; the sandwich version developed in the United States in the late 1800s."],
  ["what is a hot dog | where did hot dogs come from", "A **hot dog** is a cooked sausage (like a frankfurter or wiener) served in a split bun. Sausages like these came from Germany and Austria (Frankfurt and Vienna), and German immigrants made them popular in the US in the 1800s."],
  ["what is a national dish | what does national dish mean | national dishes", "A **national dish** is a food strongly connected to a country's identity and culture. Some are official, but most are simply widely recognized by tradition — and many countries have more than one candidate!"],
  ["what is the national dish of italy | italy national dish | famous italian food", "Italy has no single official national dish, but **pizza** (especially Neapolitan Margherita), **pasta** dishes like spaghetti al ragù, and **risotto** are its most famous foods. Italian cooking is very regional."],
  ["what is the national dish of france | france national dish | famous french food", "France's most often named national dish is **pot-au-feu**, a slow-simmered beef and vegetable stew. Other famous French dishes include coq au vin, boeuf bourguignon, ratatouille, crêpes and croissants."],
  ["what is the national dish of spain | spain national dish | famous spanish food", "**Paella** from Valencia is Spain's best-known dish, though the **Spanish tortilla** (potato omelette) and **jamón** (cured ham) are just as beloved. Tapas culture is also a big part of Spanish eating."],
  ["what is the national dish of mexico | mexico national dish | famous mexican food", "**Mole poblano**, a rich chili-and-chocolate sauce served over turkey or chicken, is often called Mexico's national dish. Tacos, tamales and chiles en nogada are also iconic. Traditional Mexican cuisine is on UNESCO's cultural heritage list."],
  ["what is the national dish of japan | japan national dish | famous japanese food", "Japan's most iconic dishes are **sushi**, **ramen** and Japanese **curry rice** — curry rice is so popular that many call it the true national dish. Washoku (traditional Japanese cuisine) is recognized by UNESCO."],
  ["what is the national dish of china | china national dish | famous chinese food", "China is huge, with many regional cuisines (like Sichuan, Cantonese and Shandong). **Peking duck** is often called its national dish, and **dumplings (jiaozi)**, noodles and rice are staples everywhere."],
  ["what is the national dish of india | india national dish | famous indian food", "India has no official national dish because its food varies so much by region. **Khichdi** (rice and lentils) is sometimes named, and dishes like biryani, dal, butter chicken and dosa are famous nationwide."],
  ["what is the national dish of the united states | america national dish | famous american food", "The US has no official national dish, but the **hamburger**, **hot dog**, **apple pie** and **barbecue** are classic American foods. Thanksgiving turkey is a big tradition too."],
  ["what is the national dish of the uk | england national dish | british national dish", "England's classic dishes include **fish and chips**, the **Sunday roast** (roast beef with Yorkshire pudding) and the full English breakfast. **Chicken tikka masala** is also famous for having been called a British national dish."],
  ["what is the national dish of scotland | scotland national dish", "Scotland's national dish is **haggis**, served with neeps and tatties (turnips and potatoes). It's especially eaten on Burns Night, January 25, celebrating poet Robert Burns."],
  ["what is the national dish of ireland | ireland national dish | irish food", "**Irish stew** — lamb or mutton slowly cooked with potatoes, onions and carrots — is Ireland's national dish. Soda bread, colcannon (mashed potatoes with cabbage) and boxty (potato pancakes) are also classics."],
  ["what is the national dish of wales | welsh national dish | what is cawl", "Wales's national dish is **cawl**, a stew of lamb or beef with leeks, potatoes, swede and carrots. **Welsh rarebit** (a savory cheese sauce on toast) and bara brith (fruit bread) are also traditional."],
  ["what is the national dish of germany | germany national dish | famous german food", "Germany doesn't have an official national dish, but **sauerbraten** (marinated pot roast) is often named. Bratwurst, schnitzel, pretzels and sauerkraut are also famously German."],
  ["what is the national dish of greece | greece national dish | famous greek food", "**Moussaka** — layers of eggplant, spiced meat sauce and creamy béchamel, baked — is often called Greece's national dish. **Fasolada** (bean soup) is another candidate, and souvlaki, gyros and Greek salad are hugely popular."],
  ["what is the national dish of turkey | turkish national dish | famous turkish food", "Turkey's best-known dishes include **kebabs** (like döner and İskender), **köfte** meatballs, **meze** and sweets like baklava and Turkish delight. Some consider **kuru fasulye** (white bean stew with rice) the national dish."],
  ["what is the national dish of russia | russian national dish | famous russian food", "Russia's classic dishes include **pelmeni** (meat dumplings), **shchi** (cabbage soup), **borscht** and **blini**. Beef stroganoff is also a famous Russian creation."],
  ["what is the national dish of ukraine | ukraine national dish", "**Borscht**, the beet soup, is Ukraine's national dish. In 2022 UNESCO listed Ukrainian borscht cooking as cultural heritage in need of urgent safeguarding. **Varenyky** (dumplings) are another favorite."],
  ["what is the national dish of poland | poland national dish | polish food", "Poland's national dish is often named as **bigos** ('hunter's stew' of sauerkraut, cabbage and meats), though **pierogi** are the most famous Polish food worldwide."],
  ["what is the national dish of hungary | hungary national dish", "Hungary's national dish is **goulash** (gulyás), a paprika-spiced beef soup-stew. Chicken paprikash is another famous Hungarian dish."],
  ["what is the national dish of switzerland | switzerland national dish | swiss food", "Switzerland is known for **cheese fondue** and **raclette** (melted cheese scraped over potatoes), and **rösti**, a crispy potato pancake, is often called the national dish."],
  ["what is the national dish of austria | austria national dish", "Austria's national dish is **Wiener schnitzel** — a thin veal cutlet breaded and fried, served with lemon. Apple strudel and Sachertorte are famous Austrian desserts."],
  ["what is the national dish of the netherlands | dutch national dish | dutch food", "The Netherlands' classic national dish is **stamppot**, mashed potatoes mixed with vegetables like kale, served with smoked sausage. Stroopwafels, herring and poffertjes (mini pancakes) are also beloved."],
  ["what is the national dish of belgium | belgium national dish | belgian food", "Belgium's national dish is **moules-frites** — mussels with fries. Belgium is also famous for waffles, chocolate and fries (Belgians say they invented them!)."],
  ["what is the national dish of portugal | portugal national dish", "Portugal is famous for **bacalhau** (salted dried cod), said to have hundreds of recipes. **Caldo verde** (kale and potato soup) and **pastéis de nata** (custard tarts) are also national favorites."],
  ["what is the national dish of sweden | sweden national dish | swedish food", "Swedish favorites include **Swedish meatballs** (köttbullar) with lingonberry jam, mashed potatoes and cream sauce. Pickled herring and cinnamon buns are also iconic."],
  ["what is the national dish of norway | norway national dish", "Norway's national dish is **fårikål**, a simple stew of mutton and cabbage cooked with whole black peppercorns. It even has its own day — the last Thursday in September."],
  ["what is the national dish of canada | canada national dish | canadian food", "**Poutine** is the dish most people think of as Canada's national food. Maple syrup, butter tarts and Nanaimo bars are also proudly Canadian."],
  ["what is the national dish of brazil | brazil national dish", "Brazil's national dish is **feijoada**, a black bean stew with pork and beef, served with rice, collard greens, orange slices and farofa."],
  ["what is the national dish of argentina | argentina national dish", "Argentina is famous for **asado** — a barbecue of beef and other meats cooked over wood or charcoal — served with chimichurri sauce. Empanadas and dulce de leche are also beloved."],
  ["what is the national dish of peru | peru national dish", "Peru's national dish is **ceviche** — raw fish marinated in lime juice with onion and chili. Peru is also famous for lomo saltado and its thousands of potato varieties."],
  ["what is the national dish of colombia | colombia national dish", "Colombia's national dish is often named as **ajiaco**, a chicken and potato soup from Bogotá, or the **bandeja paisa**, a huge platter of beans, rice, meat, plantain, avocado and egg. Arepas are eaten everywhere."],
  ["what is the national dish of cuba | cuba national dish", "Cuba's national dish is **ropa vieja** ('old clothes'), shredded beef stewed in a tomato sauce with peppers and onions, served with rice and black beans."],
  ["what is the national dish of jamaica | jamaica national dish", "Jamaica's national dish is **ackee and saltfish** — the buttery fruit ackee sautéed with salted cod, onions, peppers and spices. Jerk chicken is also world-famous. (Ackee must be properly ripened and prepared, as unripe ackee is toxic.)"],
  ["what is the national dish of south korea | korea national dish | famous korean food", "**Kimchi** is Korea's most important national food, and **bibimbap** and **bulgogi** are also iconic. Kimjang, the tradition of making kimchi together for winter, is recognized by UNESCO."],
  ["what is the national dish of thailand | thailand national dish", "**Pad thai** is often called Thailand's national dish, while **tom yum** soup and **green curry** are just as famous. Thai food balances sweet, sour, salty and spicy flavors."],
  ["what is the national dish of vietnam | vietnam national dish", "**Phở**, the fragrant noodle soup, is Vietnam's best-known national dish. Bánh mì sandwiches and fresh spring rolls are also famous."],
  ["what is the national dish of the philippines | philippines national dish", "**Adobo** — chicken or pork braised in vinegar, soy sauce, garlic and bay leaves — is widely considered the Philippines' national dish. Lechon (whole roast pig) and sinigang (sour soup) are also beloved."],
  ["what is the national dish of indonesia | indonesia national dish", "Indonesia officially promotes several national dishes, including **nasi goreng** (fried rice), **satay**, **rendang** (slow-cooked spiced beef), **soto** and **gado-gado**. Tumpeng, a cone of rice, is a celebration dish."],
  ["what is the national dish of malaysia | malaysia national dish", "Malaysia's national dish is **nasi lemak** — rice cooked in coconut milk, served with spicy sambal, fried anchovies, peanuts, cucumber and egg."],
  ["what is the national dish of singapore | singapore national dish", "**Hainanese chicken rice** — poached chicken with fragrant rice cooked in chicken broth, plus chili and ginger sauces — is Singapore's unofficial national dish. Chili crab and laksa are also famous."],
  ["what is the national dish of australia | australia national dish | australian food", "Australia doesn't have an official dish, but the **meat pie**, **pavlova**, **lamingtons**, **Vegemite on toast** and barbecued 'snags' (sausages) are national favorites."],
  ["what is the national dish of new zealand | new zealand national dish", "New Zealand is known for **pavlova**, roast lamb and the Māori **hāngī** — food cooked in an earth oven using heated stones buried in a pit."],
  ["what is the national dish of ethiopia | ethiopia national dish", "Ethiopia's national dish is **doro wat**, a spicy chicken stew made with berbere spice and hard-boiled eggs, served on spongy **injera** bread."],
  ["what is the national dish of nigeria | nigeria national dish", "**Jollof rice** is Nigeria's most famous dish, though **egusi soup** with pounded yam is another strong contender for the national dish title."],
  ["what is the national dish of morocco | morocco national dish", "Morocco is famous for **couscous** (traditionally eaten on Fridays) and **tagine**, slow-cooked stews named after their cone-lidded clay pot. Mint tea is the national drink."],
  ["what is the national dish of egypt | egypt national dish", "Egypt's national dish is **koshari** — a mix of rice, lentils, macaroni and chickpeas topped with spicy tomato sauce and crispy fried onions. **Ful medames** (stewed fava beans) is a classic breakfast."],
  ["what is the national dish of south africa | south africa national dish", "South Africa's national dish is often named as **bobotie**, a baked dish of spiced minced meat with an egg topping. **Braai** (barbecue) culture and **bunny chow** (curry in a hollowed bread loaf) are also iconic."],
  ["what is the national dish of israel | israel national dish", "**Falafel** is often called Israel's national dish, though it's eaten across the Middle East. Hummus and shakshuka are also hugely popular."],
  ["what is the national dish of lebanon | lebanon national dish", "Lebanon's national dish is **kibbeh**, made from bulgur wheat and minced meat formed into shells and stuffed, then fried or baked. **Tabbouleh** is also a national favorite."],
  ["what is the national dish of iran | iran national dish | persian food", "Iran's national dish is often considered **chelow kabab** — grilled kebab served with buttery saffron rice. **Ghormeh sabzi**, an herb stew, is also hugely popular."],
  ["what is the national dish of pakistan | pakistan national dish", "Pakistan's national dish is **nihari**, a slow-cooked beef or lamb stew traditionally eaten for breakfast. Biryani is also extremely popular."],
  ["what is the national dish of nepal | nepal national dish | what are momos", "Nepal's everyday national meal is **dal bhat** — lentil soup with rice, often with vegetable curry and pickles. **Momos** (steamed dumplings) are Nepal's favorite snack."],
  ["what is the national dish of hawaii | hawaiian food | what is poke | what is poi", "Classic Hawaiian foods include **poke** (cubed raw fish seasoned with soy sauce, sesame and seaweed), **poi** (mashed taro root), **kalua pig** cooked in an underground oven, and **spam musubi**."],
  ["what does saute mean | how to saute | what is sauteing", "To **sauté** means to cook food quickly in a small amount of fat over fairly high heat, stirring or tossing it. The word comes from the French for 'jump' because the food is tossed in the pan. Ask an adult to help at the stove."],
  ["what does braise mean | what is braising | how to braise meat", "**Braising** means browning food first, then cooking it slowly in a covered pot with a little liquid (like broth) at low heat. It turns tough cuts of meat tender — pot roast and short ribs are braised."],
  ["what does poach mean", "**Poaching** is cooking food gently in liquid that's hot but not boiling — about 160–180°F (70–82°C). It's used for delicate foods like eggs, fish and pears."],
  ["what does roast mean | what is roasting | roasting vs baking", "**Roasting** and **baking** both cook food with dry oven heat. 'Roasting' is usually used for meats and vegetables at higher temperatures to brown them, while 'baking' is used for breads, cakes, cookies and casseroles."],
  ["what does blanch mean | what is blanching | how to blanch vegetables", "**Blanching** means briefly boiling food (usually vegetables) and then plunging it into ice water to stop the cooking. It keeps vegetables bright and crisp, loosens tomato and peach skins, and prepares veggies for freezing."],
  ["what does caramelize mean | how to caramelize onions | what is caramelization", "**Caramelizing** is browning sugar with heat, which creates rich, sweet, nutty flavors. Caramelized onions are cooked slowly in butter or oil over low heat for 30–45 minutes, stirring often, until deep golden and sweet."],
  ["what does emulsify mean | what is an emulsion | how to emulsify", "To **emulsify** is to mix two liquids that normally don't blend, like oil and vinegar, into a smooth mixture. You whisk hard while adding the oil slowly. Ingredients like mustard or egg yolk act as **emulsifiers** to keep it from separating — that's how mayonnaise works!"],
  ["what does knead mean | how to knead dough | how long to knead bread", "**Kneading** means pressing, folding and turning dough with the heels of your hands to develop gluten so bread becomes stretchy and rises well. Push the dough away, fold it back, turn it a quarter turn, and repeat for about 8–10 minutes until it's smooth and springs back when poked."],
  ["what does fold mean in baking | how to fold ingredients | folding in egg whites", "To **fold** is to gently combine a light, airy mixture (like whipped cream or egg whites) into a heavier one without knocking out the air. Use a spatula to cut down through the middle, sweep along the bottom and lift over the top, turning the bowl as you go."],
  ["what does proof mean | proofing dough | what is proofing yeast", "**Proofing** is letting yeast dough rise before baking, usually in a warm spot until it's about doubled. 'Proofing yeast' also means checking it's alive by mixing it with warm water and sugar to see if it foams."],
  ["what does sear mean | how to sear meat | does searing seal in juices", "**Searing** means browning the surface of food in a very hot pan to create a flavorful crust. It doesn't actually 'seal in juices' (that's a myth), but it adds lots of flavor through browning. Always have an adult help — hot oil can splatter."],
  ["what does deglaze mean | how to deglaze a pan", "**Deglazing** means pouring a liquid (like broth, wine or water) into a hot pan after cooking meat, then scraping up the browned bits stuck to the bottom. Those bits (called **fond**) are packed with flavor and become the base for a sauce."],
  ["simmer vs boil | what is a simmer", "A **simmer** is when liquid is hot with small, gentle bubbles rising — about 185–205°F (85–96°C). A **boil** is big, rolling bubbles at 212°F (100°C) at sea level. Soups and sauces are usually simmered so they don't break apart or burn."],
  ["what does broil mean | what is broiling | how to use the broiler", "**Broiling** cooks food with intense heat from the top of the oven, like an upside-down grill. It's great for melting cheese and browning tops quickly, but food can burn in seconds, so watch closely and use oven mitts."],
  ["what does grill mean | what is grilling | grilling vs barbecue", "**Grilling** cooks food quickly over direct, high heat, like burgers and hot dogs. **Barbecuing** (in the American sense) cooks food low and slow with indirect heat and smoke for hours, like ribs or brisket."],
  ["what does steam mean | what is steaming food", "**Steaming** cooks food with the hot vapor from boiling water, usually in a basket above the water with a lid on. It's gentle and keeps vegetables bright and full of nutrients."],
  ["what does stir fry mean | what is stir frying | how to stir fry", "**Stir-frying** is a Chinese technique of cooking small, evenly cut pieces of food very quickly in a hot wok or pan with a little oil, stirring constantly. Have all your ingredients chopped before you start — it goes fast!"],
  ["what does deep fry mean | what is deep frying | is deep frying safe", "**Deep-frying** means fully submerging food in hot oil, usually 350–375°F (175–190°C). It makes food crispy fast, but hot oil is very dangerous, so it's strictly an adult job. Never put water on an oil fire — cover it with a lid and turn off the heat."],
  ["what does marinate mean | how long to marinate | what is a marinade", "To **marinate** is to soak food in a seasoned liquid (a **marinade**) to add flavor, often with acid like citrus or vinegar plus oil and spices. Most meats need 30 minutes to a few hours; fish only 15–30 minutes. Always marinate in the fridge, and don't reuse marinade from raw meat unless it's boiled."],
  ["what does brine mean | what is brining | how to brine a turkey", "**Brining** means soaking meat (often poultry or pork) in salty water for several hours. The salt seasons the meat and helps it hold onto moisture, so it stays juicy. Keep it cold in the fridge while it brines."],
  ["what does reduce mean in cooking | what is a reduction | reduce a sauce", "To **reduce** a liquid means simmering it uncovered so water evaporates. This thickens it and concentrates the flavor. A sauce made this way is called a **reduction**."],
  ["what does whisk mean | how to whisk | whisking vs beating", "To **whisk** means to mix quickly with a whisk or fork, adding air and making things smooth — like eggs or dressings. **Beating** is similar but more vigorous, often with a mixer, like beating egg whites or creaming butter."],
  ["what does dice mean | dice vs chop vs mince | what is mincing", "These are knife cuts by size: **chop** means rough pieces, **dice** means neat even cubes, and **mince** means cutting into very tiny pieces (like garlic). Always ask an adult before using sharp knives."],
  ["what is julienne | julienne cut | how to julienne", "**Julienne** is a knife cut that makes thin, matchstick-shaped strips, often of carrots, peppers or zucchini. It's great for stir-fries and salads. Little cooks can use a julienne peeler with an adult's help."],
  ["how to zest a lemon | what is citrus zest", "**Zest** is the colorful outer skin of citrus fruits like lemons, limes and oranges. It's full of flavorful oils. Rub the fruit gently on a fine grater (like a Microplane) and stop before the bitter white layer (the pith)."],
  ["what does temper mean in cooking | tempering eggs | tempering chocolate", "**Tempering eggs** means slowly whisking a little hot liquid into beaten eggs to warm them gently, so they don't scramble when added to a hot mixture. **Tempering chocolate** means carefully heating and cooling it so it sets shiny and snaps cleanly."],
  ["al dente pasta", "**Al dente** is Italian for 'to the tooth'. It describes pasta (or vegetables) cooked until tender but still slightly firm when you bite it. Taste a piece a minute or two before the package time."],
  ["what is a roux | how to make a roux", "A **roux** is equal parts fat (usually butter) and flour cooked together, used to thicken sauces, soups and gravies. A light roux cooks 1–2 minutes for white sauces; a dark roux cooks much longer for gumbo, turning nutty and brown."],
  ["what is bechamel | bechamel sauce | white sauce recipe", "**Béchamel** is a classic French white sauce. Melt 2 tbsp butter, whisk in 2 tbsp flour and cook 1 minute, then slowly whisk in 1 cup warm milk and simmer until thick. Season with salt and nutmeg. It's used in lasagna, mac and cheese and croque monsieur."],
  ["what are the mother sauces | five french mother sauces", "The five French **mother sauces** are **béchamel** (milk + roux), **velouté** (light stock + roux), **espagnole** (brown stock + roux), **hollandaise** (egg yolk + butter emulsion) and **sauce tomate** (tomato). Many other sauces are made from these."],
  ["what is mise en place | mise en place meaning", "**Mise en place** (French for 'everything in its place') means getting all your ingredients measured, chopped and ready and your tools set out before you start cooking. It makes cooking calmer and helps you avoid mistakes."],
  ["what does baste mean | what is basting", "To **baste** is to spoon or brush melted fat, juices or a sauce over food while it cooks, often meat like a roasting turkey. It adds flavor and helps the outside brown."],
  ["what does score mean in cooking | scoring bread | why do you score bread", "**Scoring** means making shallow cuts on the surface of food. Bakers score bread dough so it expands in a controlled way in the oven instead of bursting randomly — and it makes pretty patterns!"],
  ["what is sous vide | sous vide cooking", "**Sous vide** (French for 'under vacuum') is a method where food is sealed in a bag and cooked in a water bath held at a precise temperature. It cooks food very evenly; meats are often seared afterward for color."],
  ["what does flambe mean | what is flambe", "To **flambé** means pouring alcohol over food in a pan and lighting it so the alcohol burns off in a burst of flames, as in crêpes Suzette or bananas Foster. It's a dramatic technique for trained adults only."],
  ["what does pickle mean | how to quick pickle | quick pickled onions", "**Pickling** preserves food in vinegar or salty brine. For quick pickled onions: thinly slice a red onion, put it in a jar, cover with 1/2 cup vinegar, 1/2 cup hot water, 1 tbsp sugar and 1 tsp salt, and refrigerate for at least 30 minutes. They keep about 2 weeks in the fridge."],
  ["what is fermentation in food | what are fermented foods | what does ferment mean", "**Fermentation** is when microbes like bacteria and yeast change food, often turning sugars into acids, gas or alcohol. It preserves food and creates new flavors. Yogurt, cheese, bread, kimchi, sauerkraut, soy sauce, miso and chocolate all involve fermentation."],
  ["what does toast spices mean | how to toast nuts | toasting spices", "**Toasting** spices, nuts or seeds means heating them in a dry pan over medium heat for a few minutes, stirring, until fragrant and slightly darker. It releases their oils and makes them much more flavorful. They burn fast, so don't walk away!"],
  ["what does season to taste mean | season to taste", "**Season to taste** means adding salt, pepper or other seasonings a little at a time, tasting as you go, until it tastes right to you. It's always easier to add more than to take some out."],
  ["what does rest meat mean | why rest meat after cooking | why let steak rest", "**Resting** means letting cooked meat sit for 5–15 minutes before cutting. The juices settle and redistribute, so less runs out onto the cutting board. Bigger roasts need longer rests."],
  ["what does cut against the grain mean | slicing against the grain", "Meat is made of long muscle fibers called the **grain**. Cutting **against the grain** means slicing across those lines instead of along them, which makes each bite shorter and much more tender."],
  ["what is a whisk | what is a whisk used for", "A **whisk** is a tool made of looped wires on a handle. It's used to beat eggs, whip cream, mix batters smoothly and add air to mixtures."],
  ["what is a spatula | spatula vs turner | types of spatulas", "'Spatula' can mean two tools: a **flat turner** for flipping pancakes and burgers, or a **rubber/silicone spatula** for scraping bowls and folding batters. Offset spatulas are thin metal blades for spreading frosting."],
  ["what is a ladle | what is a ladle used for", "A **ladle** is a big, deep spoon with a long handle, used for scooping and serving soups, stews and sauces."],
  ["what is a colander | colander vs strainer", "A **colander** is a bowl with large holes used to drain pasta and rinse fruits and vegetables. A **strainer** (or sieve) has fine mesh, used for sifting flour or straining small things like rice or seeds."],
  ["what is a dutch oven | dutch oven", "A **Dutch oven** is a heavy pot with a tight-fitting lid, usually cast iron (often enamel-coated). It holds heat well and goes from stovetop to oven — perfect for stews, braises, soups and baking bread."],
  ["what is a cast iron skillet | how to care for cast iron | seasoning cast iron", "A **cast iron skillet** is a heavy pan that holds heat very well. Care tips: wash with hot water (a little soap is okay), dry it right away on the stove, and rub on a thin layer of oil. This builds a natural nonstick coating called **seasoning**. Avoid soaking it — it can rust."],
  ["what is a wok | wok", "A **wok** is a round-bottomed pan from China with tall, sloping sides. It heats quickly and lets you toss food easily, making it perfect for stir-frying, steaming and deep-frying."],
  ["what is a mandoline | mandoline slicer", "A **mandoline** is a slicer with a very sharp blade that cuts fruits and vegetables into thin, even slices. It's extremely sharp — always use the hand guard, and kids should only use one with an adult."],
  ["what is a mortar and pestle | mortar and pestle", "A **mortar and pestle** is an ancient tool: a heavy bowl (the mortar) and a club-shaped grinder (the pestle). It's used to crush spices, herbs and garlic, and to make pastes like pesto and guacamole."],
  ["what is a rolling pin | rolling pin", "A **rolling pin** is a cylinder of wood, marble or silicone used to flatten dough evenly for cookies, pies, pizza and pasta. Dust it and the counter with flour so the dough doesn't stick."],
  ["what is a food processor | food processor vs blender", "A **food processor** has a wide bowl and S-shaped blade for chopping, slicing, shredding and making doughs or thick dips. A **blender** has a tall jar and is best for liquids like smoothies and soups. Both have sharp blades — unplug before reaching inside."],
  ["what is a stand mixer | stand mixer vs hand mixer", "A **stand mixer** is a heavy mixer that sits on the counter with its own bowl and attachments (paddle, whisk, dough hook). A **hand mixer** is lighter and you hold it. Stand mixers are great for bread dough and big batches."],
  ["what is an immersion blender | stick blender", "An **immersion blender** (stick blender) is a hand-held blender you put right into a pot or cup. It's great for blending soups smooth without moving hot liquid into a blender. Keep the blade fully submerged to avoid splatters."],
  ["what is a slow cooker | crock pot | how does a slow cooker work", "A **slow cooker** (like a Crock-Pot) is an electric pot that cooks food at a low, steady temperature for many hours. You add ingredients in the morning and dinner is ready later — great for stews, chili and pulled meats."],
  ["what is a pressure cooker | instant pot | how does a pressure cooker work", "A **pressure cooker** seals tightly so steam builds up pressure inside. Higher pressure raises water's boiling point to about 250°F (121°C), so food cooks much faster. An **Instant Pot** is an electric multi-cooker with a pressure-cooking mode. Follow the instructions carefully when releasing steam."],
  ["what is an air fryer | how does an air fryer work | is an air fryer a fryer", "An **air fryer** is really a small convection oven. A powerful fan blows very hot air around food, making it crispy with little or no oil. Don't overcrowd the basket so the air can circulate."],
  ["what is a convection oven | convection vs regular oven", "A **convection oven** has a fan that circulates hot air, so food cooks faster and browns more evenly. A common rule is to lower the temperature by about 25°F (15°C) from a regular recipe or check food earlier."],
  ["what is a meat thermometer | how to use a food thermometer", "A **food thermometer** tells you the temperature inside food, which is the best way to know if meat is safely cooked. Insert it into the thickest part, away from bone, fat or gristle, and wait for the reading to settle."],
  ["what is a zester | microplane | what is a grater", "A **grater** has sharp-edged holes for shredding cheese and vegetables. A **Microplane** or zester is a fine grater for citrus zest, hard cheese, garlic, ginger and nutmeg. Watch your fingertips — they're sharp!"],
  ["what is a peeler | how to use a vegetable peeler", "A **vegetable peeler** has a slotted blade that removes thin strips of skin from carrots, potatoes and apples. Hold the vegetable firmly and peel **away** from your body. Kids can learn with an adult's help."],
  ["what is a chef's knife | types of kitchen knives", "Common kitchen knives: a **chef's knife** (all-purpose chopping), a **paring knife** (small, for peeling and trimming), a **serrated bread knife** (for bread and tomatoes) and a **santoku** (Japanese all-purpose knife). A sharp knife is actually safer than a dull one because it doesn't slip as easily."],
  ["what is a cutting board | wood vs plastic cutting board", "A **cutting board** protects your counter and knives. Both wood and plastic work well. Many cooks use a separate board for raw meat to avoid cross-contamination. Put a damp towel underneath so it doesn't slide."],
  ["what is a sheet pan | sheet pan vs cookie sheet", "A **sheet pan** (rimmed baking sheet) has raised edges on all four sides, great for roasting vegetables. A **cookie sheet** often has no rims (or just one) so cookies slide off easily."],
  ["what is a springform pan | springform pan", "A **springform pan** is a round cake pan with sides held by a latch. When you open it, the sides come away from the bottom, so delicate cakes like cheesecake come out without being flipped."],
  ["what is a bundt pan | bundt cake", "A **Bundt pan** is a ring-shaped pan with fluted (ridged) sides. The hole in the middle helps heat reach the center of a dense cake so it bakes evenly. Grease every groove well!"],
  ["what is a ramekin | ramekin", "A **ramekin** is a small, round, oven-safe dish, used for single servings of things like crème brûlée, soufflés, custards and dips."],
  ["what is a pastry bag | piping bag | how to pipe frosting", "A **piping bag** is a cone-shaped bag fitted with a metal tip, used to squeeze frosting, cream or dough into decorative shapes. No piping bag? Fill a zip-top bag and snip off a tiny corner."],
  ["what is a bench scraper | dough scraper", "A **bench scraper** is a flat rectangle of metal or plastic used to cut and lift dough, scrape the counter clean, and scoop chopped food from a cutting board into a pan."],
  ["what is a salad spinner | salad spinner", "A **salad spinner** is a bowl with a basket inside that spins to fling water off washed lettuce and herbs. Dry greens help dressing stick and keep salads crisp."],
  ["what is a nonstick pan | nonstick pan care", "A **nonstick pan** has a slippery coating so food releases easily — great for eggs and pancakes. To protect it, use wooden or silicone utensils, avoid very high heat, and hand-wash gently."],
  ["what is a kitchen scale | why use a kitchen scale", "A **kitchen scale** weighs ingredients. Weighing is more accurate than cups, especially for flour, which can vary a lot depending on how it's scooped. Many bakers measure in grams for consistent results."],
  ["teaspoons per tablespoon", "There are **3 teaspoons in 1 tablespoon**."],
  ["tablespoons per cup", "There are **16 tablespoons in 1 cup** (so 1/4 cup = 4 tablespoons)."],
  ["cups in a pint | cups in a gallon", "In US measurements: **2 cups = 1 pint**, **4 cups = 1 quart**, and **16 cups = 1 gallon** (4 quarts = 1 gallon)."],
  ["how many ounces in a cup | fluid ounces in a cup", "A US cup holds **8 fluid ounces** (about 240 ml). Note that fluid ounces measure volume, not weight — a cup of flour weighs only about 4.25 oz (120 g)."],
  ["how many grams in a cup of flour | cup of flour in grams | cup of sugar in grams", "Approximate weights for 1 US cup: all-purpose flour about **120–125 g**, granulated sugar about **200 g**, brown sugar (packed) about **210–220 g**, butter about **227 g** (2 sticks), and water about **240 g**."],
  ["how many ml in a teaspoon | ml in a tablespoon | metric spoon measurements", "A teaspoon is about **5 ml** and a tablespoon is about **15 ml**. A US cup is about **240 ml** (metric cups used in Australia and elsewhere are 250 ml)."],
  ["how many tablespoons in a stick of butter | how much is a stick of butter", "One US stick of butter = **8 tablespoons** = **1/2 cup** = about **113 grams**."],
  ["how do i convert fahrenheit to celsius | oven temperature conversion | 350 f in c", "To convert °F to °C: subtract 32, then multiply by 5/9. Common oven temps: 300°F ≈ 150°C, 325°F ≈ 160°C, 350°F ≈ 175–180°C, 375°F ≈ 190°C, 400°F ≈ 200°C, 425°F ≈ 220°C, 450°F ≈ 230°C."],
  ["what does a pinch mean | how much is a pinch | how much is a dash", "A **pinch** is the small amount you can hold between your thumb and finger — roughly 1/16 teaspoon. A **dash** is a bit more, about 1/8 teaspoon. They're for small amounts of salt or spice."],
  ["how do i halve a recipe | how to double a recipe | scaling a recipe", "To halve or double a recipe, multiply every ingredient by 1/2 or 2. Tricky bits: for half an egg, beat 1 egg and use about half (roughly 2 tbsp). Pan sizes and baking times may change, so check doneness early or late."],
  ["what can i use instead of buttermilk | how to make buttermilk", "**Buttermilk substitute:** put 1 tablespoon lemon juice or white vinegar in a measuring cup, add milk to reach 1 cup, stir and wait 5–10 minutes until slightly thickened. Plain yogurt thinned with a little milk works too."],
  ["what is the difference between baking soda and baking powder | baking soda vs baking powder", "**Baking soda** is pure sodium bicarbonate — it needs an acid in the recipe (like buttermilk, yogurt, lemon or brown sugar) to make bubbles. **Baking powder** is baking soda already mixed with a dry acid, so it only needs moisture and heat. Baking soda is about 3–4 times stronger."],
  ["can i use baking powder instead of baking soda | baking soda substitute | baking powder substitute", "To replace **1 tsp baking powder**, use 1/4 tsp baking soda + 1/2 tsp cream of tartar. To replace baking soda, use about 3 times as much baking powder (e.g. 1 tsp soda → 3 tsp powder), though the taste and texture may change slightly."],
  ["how do i make a flax egg | vegan egg substitute | chia egg", "**Flax egg:** mix 1 tbsp ground flaxseed with 3 tbsp water and let it sit 5–10 minutes until gel-like. It replaces 1 egg in muffins, pancakes and cookies. A **chia egg** works the same way with chia seeds. Mashed banana (1/4 cup) or applesauce also work in sweet bakes."],
  ["what can i use instead of butter | butter substitute in baking", "In baking you can often swap butter with an equal amount of margarine or a plant-based butter. For quick breads and muffins, try oil (use about 3/4 the amount), or applesauce or mashed banana to replace some of the fat. Cookies may spread or taste different."],
  ["what can i use instead of sour cream | sour cream substitute", "**Plain Greek yogurt** is the easiest swap for sour cream, 1:1. Crème fraîche also works. For dairy-free, try blended cashews with lemon juice."],
  ["what can i use instead of heavy cream | heavy cream substitute", "For cooking (not whipping), mix **3/4 cup milk + 1/4 cup melted butter** to replace 1 cup heavy cream. Evaporated milk or half-and-half can also work in sauces and soups. For whipping, full-fat coconut cream (chilled) works."],
  ["what can i use instead of brown sugar | how to make brown sugar | brown sugar substitute", "Make **brown sugar** by mixing 1 cup white sugar with 1 tbsp molasses (light brown) or 2 tbsp molasses (dark brown). In a pinch, plain white sugar works too — cookies will be a little crisper."],
  ["what can i use instead of powdered sugar | how to make powdered sugar | icing sugar substitute", "Make **powdered sugar** by blending 1 cup granulated sugar with 1 tbsp cornstarch in a blender until very fine and fluffy. Let the dust settle before opening the lid!"],
  ["what can i use instead of cornstarch | cornstarch substitute", "To thicken, replace **1 tbsp cornstarch** with about **2 tbsp flour**, or use an equal amount of arrowroot or potato starch. Mix it with cold water first so it doesn't clump."],
  ["what can i use instead of wine in cooking | wine substitute", "Instead of wine, use **broth** (chicken, beef or vegetable) for savory dishes, plus a splash of vinegar or lemon juice for acidity. For red wine, unsweetened grape or cranberry juice with a little vinegar works too."],
  ["what can i use instead of lemon juice | lemon juice substitute", "Lime juice is the closest swap, 1:1. White wine vinegar or apple cider vinegar can work in smaller amounts (about half as much), especially in savory dishes."],
  ["what can i use instead of fresh herbs | dried vs fresh herbs | how much dried herbs for fresh", "Dried herbs are stronger, so use about **1 teaspoon dried for every 1 tablespoon fresh** (a 1:3 ratio). Add dried herbs early in cooking and fresh herbs near the end."],
  ["what can i use instead of breadcrumbs | breadcrumb substitute", "Swap **breadcrumbs** with crushed crackers, crushed cornflakes, rolled oats, panko, or crushed pork rinds. For gluten-free, try almond flour or gluten-free oats."],
  ["what can i use instead of milk | milk substitute in baking | dairy free milk", "In most recipes you can swap cow's milk 1:1 with unsweetened plant milks like oat, soy or almond milk. Water works in some recipes but makes things less rich."],
  ["what can i use instead of vanilla extract | vanilla substitute", "Try **maple syrup** (1:1), a little almond extract (use much less — it's strong), or the seeds of a vanilla bean. In many recipes, you can also simply leave vanilla out."],
  ["what can i use instead of tomato paste | tomato paste substitute", "Replace **1 tbsp tomato paste** with about **3 tbsp tomato sauce or puree**, simmered a bit longer to thicken. Ketchup works in a pinch but is sweeter."],
  ["what can i use instead of sugar | sugar substitutes in baking", "Honey or maple syrup can replace sugar: use about 3/4 cup per cup of sugar and reduce other liquids by about 1/4 cup, and lower the oven by 25°F (15°C) since they brown faster. Sugar also adds texture, so results will change a little."],
  ["what is the difference between salted and unsalted butter | salted vs unsalted butter", "**Unsalted butter** lets you control the salt in a recipe, which is why bakers prefer it. If you only have **salted butter**, reduce the added salt by about 1/4 tsp per stick (1/2 cup)."],
  ["what is the difference between white and brown sugar | white sugar vs brown sugar", "**Brown sugar** is white sugar with molasses, which adds moisture, a caramel taste and acidity. It makes cookies softer and chewier; white sugar makes them crisper and helps them spread."],
  ["what is the difference between cocoa powder and dutch process | natural vs dutch cocoa", "**Natural cocoa** is acidic and pairs with baking soda. **Dutch-process cocoa** is treated with an alkali, making it darker, milder and less acidic, so it's usually paired with baking powder."],
  ["what is cream of tartar | what is cream of tartar used for", "**Cream of tartar** is a powdery acid (potassium bitartrate) left over from winemaking. It helps stabilize whipped egg whites, keeps sugar syrups from crystallizing, and gives snickerdoodles their tang."],
  ["what is the difference between stock and broth | stock vs broth", "**Stock** is usually made by simmering bones (and vegetables) for a long time, so it's rich and slightly gelatinous. **Broth** is typically made from meat or vegetables, simmered for less time, and seasoned so you can sip it. Recipes often use them interchangeably."],
  ["what is the difference between yams and sweet potatoes | yam vs sweet potato", "True **yams** are starchy tubers from Africa and Asia with rough, bark-like skin. In US stores, orange 'yams' are usually actually soft **sweet potatoes** — the two plants aren't even closely related."],
  ["what is the difference between herbs and spices | herbs vs spices", "**Herbs** are the leafy green parts of plants (like basil, parsley and mint). **Spices** come from other parts — seeds, bark, roots, berries or flowers — such as cinnamon (bark), cumin (seeds), ginger (root) and cloves (flower buds)."],
  ["why does bread rise | what makes bread rise", "Bread rises because **yeast** — tiny living fungi — eats sugars in the dough and gives off **carbon dioxide gas**. The stretchy gluten network traps the gas in bubbles, so the dough puffs up. In the oven, the gas expands even more before the bread sets."],
  ["what makes onions make you cry | why do onions burn my eyes | why do onions sting your eyes", "Cutting an onion breaks its cells and releases chemicals that combine into a gas called **syn-propanethial-S-oxide**. When it reaches your eyes, it irritates them, and your eyes make tears to wash it away. Chilling the onion and using a sharp knife helps."],
  ["why does popcorn pop | how does popcorn pop | what makes popcorn pop", "Each popcorn kernel has a tiny bit of **water** inside a hard shell. When heated, the water turns to steam and pressure builds until the shell bursts at around 355°F (180°C). The soft starch inside puffs out and sets into the fluffy white shape."],
  ["why do cut apples go brown | how to stop apples turning brown", "When you cut an apple, an enzyme (**polyphenol oxidase**) reacts with oxygen in the air, turning chemicals in the fruit brown. To slow it, coat slices with lemon juice, dip them in lightly salted water, or keep them in an airtight container."],
  ["why do avocados turn brown | how to keep guacamole green", "Avocados brown for the same reason apples do: enzymes react with oxygen. To keep guacamole green, add lime juice, press plastic wrap right onto the surface, and refrigerate."],
  ["what is the maillard reaction | why does food turn brown when cooked | browning reaction", "The **Maillard reaction** is a chemical reaction between proteins and sugars at high heat (above about 280°F / 140°C) that browns food and creates hundreds of delicious flavors. It's why toast, seared steak, roasted coffee and baked bread taste so good."],
  ["why do eggs get hard when cooked | why do eggs solidify", "Egg proteins are long, folded molecules. Heat makes them unfold and bond to each other in a tangled net, which traps water and turns the runny egg solid. This is called **denaturing** and **coagulation** — and you can't undo it!"],
  ["why does salt make water boil faster | does salt make water boil faster", "It's actually the opposite! Adding salt slightly **raises** the boiling point of water, but the amount used in cooking is so small the change is tiny. People salt pasta water for **flavor**, not speed."],
  ["why does water boil at a lower temperature in the mountains | cooking at high altitude", "At high altitude there's less air pressure pushing down on the water, so it boils at a **lower temperature** — about 203°F (95°C) at 5,000 feet instead of 212°F (100°C). Food cooks more slowly, so pasta and boiled eggs take longer, and baking recipes often need adjusting."],
  ["why does chocolate melt in your mouth | why does chocolate melt", "Cocoa butter, the fat in chocolate, melts at around 93–97°F (34–36°C) — just below body temperature. That's why chocolate stays solid in your hand briefly but melts smoothly on your tongue."],
  ["why does ice cream give you brain freeze", "**Brain freeze** happens when something very cold touches the roof of your mouth. Blood vessels there quickly narrow and then widen, and nerves send a pain signal that feels like it's in your forehead. Pressing your tongue to the roof of your mouth can help it pass."],
  ["why are chili peppers spicy | what makes peppers hot | what is capsaicin", "Chili peppers contain **capsaicin**, a chemical that tricks heat-sensing nerves in your mouth into thinking they're burning. Most of it is in the white ribs and seeds. Milk or yogurt helps cool the burn better than water."],
  ["what is the scoville scale | hottest pepper in the world | how hot is a jalapeno", "The **Scoville scale** measures how spicy peppers are in Scoville Heat Units (SHU). A bell pepper is 0, a jalapeño is about 2,500–8,000, a habanero about 100,000–350,000, and the Carolina Reaper, one of the world's hottest, averages over 1.5 million SHU."],
  ["why does milk help with spicy food | what to drink for spicy food | how to stop mouth burning", "Capsaicin, the spicy chemical in chilies, dissolves in fat, not water. Milk has fat and a protein called **casein** that helps wash capsaicin away. Water just spreads it around! Yogurt, milk or a bite of bread or rice works best."],
  ["why does whipped cream get fluffy | how does whipping cream work", "Heavy cream has lots of fat. When you whip it, you push in air bubbles, and the fat droplets partly clump together around them, forming a stable foam. Cold cream works best because the fat stays firm. Whip too long and it turns into butter!"],
  ["how is butter made | how do you make butter | make butter in a jar", "**Butter** is made by **churning** cream until the fat globules clump together and separate from the liquid (buttermilk). Try it: shake heavy cream in a sealed jar for 10–15 minutes until a lump of butter forms, then pour off the liquid and rinse the butter in cold water."],
  ["why do beans make you gassy | why do beans make you fart", "Beans contain sugars called **oligosaccharides** that our bodies can't digest. When they reach the large intestine, bacteria there break them down and produce gas. Soaking and rinsing beans helps reduce it."],
  ["why does pineapple make my mouth tingle | why does pineapple hurt my tongue", "Pineapple contains **bromelain**, an enzyme that breaks down proteins — including those in your mouth! That's what causes the tingly feeling. Cooking pineapple destroys the enzyme. It's also why fresh pineapple stops gelatin from setting."],
  ["why is pink chicken bad | why does chicken need to be fully cooked", "Raw chicken can carry harmful bacteria like **Salmonella** and **Campylobacter**. Cooking it to **165°F (74°C)** inside kills them. Color alone isn't a reliable test, so a food thermometer is the best way to be sure."],
  ["why do bananas turn brown | why do bananas get spots", "As bananas ripen, their starch turns into sugar and they give off **ethylene gas**, which speeds ripening. Brown spots appear as the skin's green chlorophyll breaks down and enzymes cause browning. Spotty bananas are sweeter — perfect for banana bread!"],
  ["how do i ripen an avocado faster | how to ripen fruit faster | paper bag ripening", "Put the avocado (or peaches, pears, bananas) in a **paper bag** with a ripe banana or apple. They release **ethylene gas**, which speeds up ripening, and the bag traps it. Check daily."],
  ["why does soda fizz | what makes soda bubbly | why is soda fizzy", "Soda is filled with **carbon dioxide** gas dissolved under pressure. When you open the bottle, the pressure drops and the gas escapes as bubbles. Cold soda stays fizzy longer because cold liquids hold gas better."],
  ["why does sugar dissolve faster in hot water | does sugar dissolve in hot or cold water", "Sugar dissolves faster in hot water because the water molecules move faster and bump into the sugar crystals more often and harder, pulling them apart. Hot water can also hold much more dissolved sugar than cold water."],
  ["why do cookies spread", "Cookies spread when the butter is too warm or melted, there's too much sugar or baking soda, or not enough flour. Chill the dough 30 minutes, measure flour correctly, and use a cool baking sheet for thicker cookies."],
  ["five basic tastes", "The five basic tastes are **sweet, sour, salty, bitter and umami**. **Umami** is a rich, savory taste from glutamates, found in foods like parmesan, mushrooms, tomatoes, soy sauce and broth. It was identified by Japanese scientist Kikunae Ikeda in 1908."],
  ["how does taste and smell work together | why can't i taste food when i have a cold", "Much of what we call 'flavor' actually comes from **smell**. When you chew, aromas travel up the back of your throat to your nose. With a stuffy nose, those smells can't get through, so food tastes bland."],
  ["how long does honey last | does honey go bad | why doesn't honey spoil", "Honey almost never spoils because it's very low in water, very high in sugar and slightly acidic — conditions where bacteria can't grow. Archaeologists have found edible honey in ancient Egyptian tombs! It may crystallize, but warming it gently makes it liquid again. (Never give honey to babies under 1.)"],
  ["why does cheese have holes | why does swiss cheese have holes", "The holes (called **eyes**) in Swiss cheese like Emmental form when bacteria in the cheese produce **carbon dioxide** gas as it ages. The gas collects in bubbles that become holes."],
  ["why does bread go stale | how to keep bread fresh", "Bread goes stale mainly because its starch molecules recrystallize and push water out, making it hard — not just because it dries out. Store bread at room temperature in a bag or bread box, or freeze it. The fridge actually makes it stale faster!"],
  ["why does food taste better with salt | what does salt do in cooking", "Salt boosts flavors, reduces bitterness and makes sweet and savory tastes stand out. It also changes texture: it helps meat hold moisture, strengthens bread dough and draws water out of vegetables."],
  ["why do some people think cilantro tastes like soap | cilantro tastes like soap", "Some people have a gene variation (near the **OR6A2** smell receptor) that makes them very sensitive to certain aldehyde chemicals in cilantro — the same type found in soap. For them, cilantro really does taste soapy!"],
  ["what temperature should chicken be cooked to | safe temperature for chicken | chicken internal temp", "Chicken and other poultry (including ground poultry) should reach **165°F (74°C)** in the thickest part, measured with a food thermometer."],
  ["what temperature should beef be cooked to | safe temperature for ground beef | burger internal temperature", "**Ground beef, pork and lamb** should reach **160°F (71°C)**. **Whole cuts** of beef, pork, veal and lamb (steaks, roasts, chops) should reach **145°F (63°C)** and then rest for 3 minutes."],
  ["what temperature should pork be cooked to | safe temperature for pork", "Whole cuts of pork (chops, roasts, tenderloin) are safe at **145°F (63°C)** with a 3-minute rest, so they can be slightly pink inside. Ground pork needs **160°F (71°C)**."],
  ["what temperature should fish be cooked to | safe temperature for fish | how do i know fish is done", "Fish and shellfish are safe at **145°F (63°C)**. Without a thermometer, cooked fish turns opaque and flakes easily with a fork."],
  ["safe cooking temperatures | food safety temperature chart | minimum internal temperatures", "Widely published safe minimum internal temperatures:\n• Poultry (whole or ground): **165°F (74°C)**\n• Ground beef, pork, lamb: **160°F (71°C)**\n• Egg dishes: **160°F (71°C)**\n• Steaks, roasts, chops (beef, pork, lamb, veal): **145°F (63°C)** + 3-minute rest\n• Fish and shellfish: **145°F (63°C)**\n• Leftovers and casseroles: **165°F (74°C)**"],
  ["what is the danger zone for food | temperature danger zone | what temperature does bacteria grow", "The **danger zone** is **40–140°F (4–60°C)** — the range where bacteria multiply fastest. Keep cold food cold (below 40°F / 4°C) and hot food hot (above 140°F / 60°C)."],
  ["how long can food sit out | how long can leftovers stay out | two hour rule", "Don't leave perishable food out for more than **2 hours** at room temperature — or **1 hour** if it's above 90°F (32°C), like at a hot picnic. After that, throw it away."],
  ["how long do leftovers last in the fridge | how long are leftovers good for | are leftovers still good", "Most cooked leftovers are safe in the fridge for **3–4 days**. Refrigerate them within 2 hours of cooking in shallow, covered containers. If you won't eat them in time, freeze them. When in doubt, throw it out!"],
  ["how do i store leftovers | storing leftovers safely | how to cool food quickly", "To store leftovers safely:\n1. Refrigerate within **2 hours** of cooking.\n2. Divide large amounts into **shallow containers** so they cool quickly.\n3. Cover them and label with the date.\n4. Eat within 3–4 days, or freeze for longer."],
  ["how do i reheat leftovers safely | reheating food | what temperature to reheat leftovers", "Reheat leftovers until they're steaming hot all the way through — **165°F (74°C)**. In the microwave, cover the food, stir halfway, and let it stand a minute so heat spreads evenly. Bring sauces and soups to a boil."],
  ["what temperature should my fridge be | fridge temperature | freezer temperature", "Keep your refrigerator at **40°F (4°C) or below** and your freezer at **0°F (-18°C)**. An appliance thermometer can help you check."],
  ["what is cross contamination | how to avoid cross contamination", "**Cross-contamination** is when germs spread from raw foods (especially meat, poultry, seafood and eggs) to ready-to-eat foods, hands, cutting boards or utensils. Prevent it by using separate cutting boards, washing hands and surfaces with hot soapy water, and keeping raw meat on the bottom shelf of the fridge."],
  ["should i wash raw chicken | do you wash chicken before cooking", "No — food safety experts recommend **not** washing raw chicken. Rinsing it can splash bacteria around your sink and kitchen. Cooking it to 165°F (74°C) kills the germs."],
  ["can i eat raw cookie dough | is raw cookie dough safe | is raw batter safe", "Eating raw cookie dough or cake batter isn't recommended. Raw eggs can carry **Salmonella**, and raw flour can carry **E. coli** because it isn't treated to kill germs. Look for edible cookie dough made with heat-treated flour and no eggs."],
  ["how long should i wash my hands before cooking | handwashing for cooking", "Wash your hands with soap and warm water for at least **20 seconds** (about two rounds of 'Happy Birthday') before cooking, after touching raw meat, eggs or the trash, and after using the bathroom."],
  ["what does best before mean | best by vs use by | expiration date meaning", "A **'best by'** or **'best before'** date is about quality — food may be fine to eat after it if stored well. A **'use by'** date is the last recommended day for best quality and, for some foods, safety. Baby formula should always be used by its date. When food smells or looks off, throw it out."],
  ["can i refreeze thawed meat | refreezing food", "Raw food thawed **in the fridge** can be refrozen without cooking, though it may lose some quality. Food thawed in the microwave or cold water should be cooked before refreezing. Never refreeze food left out more than 2 hours."],
  ["how long does food last in the freezer | is freezer burned food safe", "Food kept at 0°F (-18°C) stays **safe indefinitely**, but quality drops over time. **Freezer burn** (dry, grayish patches) is safe to eat but can taste dry — just trim those spots off. Wrapping food tightly helps prevent it."],
  ["is moldy bread safe to eat | can i cut mold off food | what to do with moldy food", "Throw away **moldy bread** and soft foods — mold roots can spread through them invisibly. For **hard cheese** and firm vegetables like carrots, you can usually cut off at least 1 inch (2.5 cm) around the moldy spot."],
  ["what is food poisoning caused by | what bacteria cause food poisoning", "Common causes of food poisoning include bacteria such as **Salmonella**, **E. coli**, **Listeria** and **Campylobacter**, and viruses like **norovirus**. They spread through undercooked food, unwashed hands, cross-contamination and food left in the danger zone."],
  ["what are common food allergies | most common food allergens", "The most common food allergens include **milk, eggs, peanuts, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, wheat, soy** and **sesame**. Always check labels and ask about ingredients when cooking for others. Severe reactions are an emergency — call for help right away."],
  ["is it safe to eat raw eggs | can you eat raw eggs", "Raw or undercooked eggs can carry **Salmonella**. It's safest to cook eggs until the whites and yolks are firm, or to use **pasteurized** eggs in recipes that call for raw eggs, like homemade mayo or mousse."],
  ["can you put hot food in the fridge | should food cool before refrigerating", "Yes — you can put hot food in the fridge. It's safer than leaving it out. Just divide big batches into **shallow containers** so it cools quickly and doesn't warm up the other food."],
  ["is it safe to thaw meat on the counter | thawing on counter", "No — thawing meat on the counter lets the outside warm into the danger zone while the inside is still frozen. Thaw it **in the fridge**, in **cold water** (changed every 30 minutes), or in the **microwave** (then cook right away)."],
  ["what is cinnamon | where does cinnamon come from | how is cinnamon made", "**Cinnamon** is the dried inner **bark** of trees in the genus Cinnamomum. Workers peel strips of bark, which curl into 'quills' as they dry. **Ceylon cinnamon** comes mainly from Sri Lanka; **cassia** cinnamon, more common in the US, comes from China, Indonesia and Vietnam."],
  ["what is saffron | why is saffron so expensive | where does saffron come from", "**Saffron** is the dried red **stigmas** of the crocus flower *Crocus sativus*. Each flower has only three, and they're picked by hand — it takes around 150,000 flowers to make 1 kg. That's why it's the world's most expensive spice. Most saffron comes from Iran."],
  ["what is turmeric | turmeric | what is turmeric used for", "**Turmeric** is a bright orange-yellow spice made from the dried root (rhizome) of a plant in the ginger family. It gives curry and mustard their golden color and has an earthy, slightly bitter flavor. It also stains everything yellow!"],
  ["what is cumin | cumin | what does cumin taste like", "**Cumin** is a spice made from the seeds of a plant in the parsley family. It has a warm, earthy, slightly smoky flavor and is key in Mexican, Indian, Middle Eastern and North African cooking."],
  ["what is paprika | paprika | smoked paprika vs sweet paprika", "**Paprika** is made from dried, ground red peppers. **Sweet paprika** is mild, **hot paprika** has a kick, and **smoked paprika** (Spanish pimentón) is dried over oak fires for a smoky taste. Hungary and Spain are famous for paprika."],
  ["what is black pepper | where does pepper come from | what is a peppercorn", "**Black pepper** comes from **peppercorns**, the dried berries of the *Piper nigrum* vine, native to southern India. Black, white and green peppercorns all come from the same plant, picked and processed differently. Pepper was once so valuable it was called 'black gold'."],
  ["what is nutmeg | nutmeg and mace | where does nutmeg come from", "**Nutmeg** is the seed of a tropical evergreen tree originally from the Banda Islands of Indonesia. The lacy red covering around the seed is dried to make another spice called **mace**. Nutmeg is used in eggnog, pies and béchamel."],
  ["what are cloves | cloves spice | what are cloves used for", "**Cloves** are the dried, unopened **flower buds** of a tree native to Indonesia's Maluku (Spice) Islands. They have a strong, warm, sweet flavor and are used in baking, chai, ham and mulled drinks."],
  ["what is cardamom | cardamom", "**Cardamom** is a fragrant spice from the seed pods of plants in the ginger family, native to India. It has a sweet, floral, citrusy taste and is used in chai, Indian curries and rice, and Scandinavian baked goods."],
  ["what is ginger | ginger root | what is ginger used for", "**Ginger** is the knobbly underground stem (rhizome) of a tropical plant. Fresh ginger is spicy and zingy, used in stir-fries, curries and teas; dried ground ginger is used in gingerbread and cookies."],
  ["what is garlic | is garlic a vegetable | garlic facts", "**Garlic** is a bulb in the onion (allium) family, made of separate cloves. It's used as both a vegetable and a seasoning in cuisines worldwide. Raw garlic is sharp; cooked garlic turns sweet and mellow. China grows the most garlic in the world."],
  ["what is oregano | oregano", "**Oregano** is a herb in the mint family with a bold, earthy flavor. It's essential in Italian, Greek and Mexican cooking — think pizza, Greek salad and salsas. Dried oregano is often stronger than fresh."],
  ["what is basil | basil | types of basil", "**Basil** is a fragrant herb in the mint family. **Sweet basil** is used in Italian dishes like pesto and caprese; **Thai basil** has an anise flavor and purple stems. Add it at the end of cooking because heat dulls its flavor."],
  ["what is cilantro | cilantro vs coriander | is cilantro the same as coriander", "**Cilantro** and **coriander** come from the same plant. In the US, 'cilantro' means the fresh leaves and 'coriander' means the dried seeds. In the UK and many other countries, the leaves are also called coriander."],
  ["what is rosemary | rosemary herb", "**Rosemary** is a woody herb with needle-like leaves and a strong pine-like scent, native to the Mediterranean. It's great with roast potatoes, chicken, lamb and focaccia."],
  ["what is thyme | thyme herb", "**Thyme** is a small-leaved Mediterranean herb with an earthy, slightly lemony flavor. It's used in soups, stews, roasts and French herb blends like herbes de Provence and bouquet garni."],
  ["what is parsley | parsley | curly vs flat leaf parsley", "**Parsley** is a bright, fresh herb. **Flat-leaf (Italian) parsley** has more flavor and is best for cooking; **curly parsley** is milder and often used as a garnish. It's the star of tabbouleh and chimichurri."],
  ["what is dill | dill herb", "**Dill** is a feathery herb with a fresh, tangy flavor. It's used in pickles, potato salad, tzatziki, fish dishes and many Scandinavian and Eastern European recipes."],
  ["what is mint | mint herb | types of mint", "**Mint** is a cooling herb. **Spearmint** is sweet and used in cooking and drinks; **peppermint** is stronger and used in candies and teas. It's great in mint tea, mojito-style mocktails, salads and Middle Eastern dishes."],
  ["what is a bay leaf | bay leaves | do bay leaves do anything", "**Bay leaves** are dried leaves of the bay laurel tree. They add a subtle herbal depth to soups, stews and sauces during long cooking. They stay tough and sharp, so remove them before serving."],
  ["what is vanilla | where does vanilla come from | why is vanilla expensive", "**Vanilla** comes from the seed pods of a tropical **orchid** native to Mexico. Each flower opens for only about a day and, outside Mexico, must be pollinated **by hand**. The pods are then cured for months. Madagascar grows most of the world's vanilla today."],
  ["what is garam masala | garam masala", "**Garam masala** is a warming Indian spice blend, often including cinnamon, cardamom, cloves, cumin, coriander and black pepper. It's usually added near the end of cooking."],
  ["what is chili powder | chili powder vs chilli powder | chili powder vs cayenne", "In the US, **chili powder** is a blend of ground dried chilies with cumin, garlic and oregano. **Cayenne** is pure ground hot red pepper and is much spicier. In many other countries, 'chilli powder' means just ground chilies."],
  ["what is allspice | allspice", "**Allspice** is the dried berry of a Caribbean tree. It's one spice — not a blend — but it tastes like a mix of cinnamon, nutmeg and cloves. It's a key ingredient in Jamaican jerk seasoning."],
  ["what is star anise | star anise", "**Star anise** is the star-shaped dried fruit of a Chinese evergreen tree. It has a strong licorice flavor and is used in Chinese five-spice powder, pho broth and braised dishes."],
  ["what is five spice powder | chinese five spice", "**Chinese five-spice powder** usually blends star anise, cloves, Chinese cinnamon (cassia), Sichuan pepper and fennel seeds. It balances sweet, warm and savory flavors and is used with pork and duck."],
  ["what is za'atar | zaatar", "**Za'atar** is a Middle Eastern spice blend, usually made of dried thyme or oregano, toasted sesame seeds, tangy sumac and salt. It's sprinkled on flatbread with olive oil, on eggs and on roasted vegetables."],
  ["what is sumac spice | sumac", "**Sumac** is a deep red spice made from dried, ground berries of the sumac bush. It has a tangy, lemony flavor and is used in Middle Eastern dishes like fattoush salad and kebabs. (It's different from poison sumac!)"],
  ["what is mustard made from | how is mustard made | mustard seeds", "**Mustard** is made from **mustard seeds** ground and mixed with liquid like water, vinegar or wine. Yellow mustard gets its bright color from turmeric; Dijon mustard comes from Dijon, France."],
  ["what is soy sauce made from | how is soy sauce made", "**Soy sauce** is made by fermenting soybeans and roasted wheat with a mold called **koji**, salt and water for months. The liquid is then pressed out. It originated in China over 2,000 years ago."],
  ["what is msg | is msg bad for you | monosodium glutamate", "**MSG** (monosodium glutamate) is a salt of glutamate, the compound that gives foods like tomatoes, parmesan and mushrooms their savory **umami** taste. Food safety agencies like the US FDA consider it generally safe to eat in normal amounts."],
  ["what is the hottest spice | what spices are hot", "Spicy heat in food mostly comes from **chili peppers** (like cayenne, chipotle and habanero). Black pepper, ginger, mustard, horseradish and wasabi also create heat, but in different ways — wasabi and horseradish hit your nose!"],
  ["what is wasabi | is wasabi horseradish", "Real **wasabi** is grated from the root-like stem of a Japanese plant, *Wasabia japonica*. It's rare and expensive, so most 'wasabi' served outside Japan is actually **horseradish** mixed with mustard and green coloring."],
  ["what is the difference between a fruit and a vegetable | fruit vs vegetable | how do you tell a fruit from a vegetable", "Botanically, a **fruit** is the part of a plant that grows from a flower and contains seeds. A **vegetable** is any other edible part — roots (carrots), stems (celery), leaves (lettuce) or flowers (broccoli). In cooking, sweet fruits are called fruits, and savory ones (like tomatoes, peppers and cucumbers) are treated as vegetables."],
  ["is a cucumber a fruit | is a pepper a fruit | is an avocado a fruit", "Yes — botanically, cucumbers, bell peppers, avocados, pumpkins, eggplants and even green beans are all **fruits**, because they grow from flowers and contain seeds. Cooks just call them vegetables because they're savory."],
  ["is a strawberry a berry | is a banana a berry | what counts as a berry", "Surprisingly, botanists say **bananas, grapes, tomatoes, kiwis and even avocados are berries**, but **strawberries and raspberries aren't**! A true berry comes from one flower with one ovary and has seeds inside. Strawberries have their seeds on the outside."],
  ["is a peanut a nut | are peanuts nuts | what is a peanut", "No — a **peanut** is a **legume**, related to beans and peas. Peanuts grow underground in pods. Even so, many people are allergic to peanuts, so always check before sharing them."],
  ["is corn a vegetable or grain | is corn a vegetable", "Corn can be all three! Fresh sweet corn is eaten as a **vegetable**, dried corn kernels (like popcorn and cornmeal) are a **grain**, and botanically each kernel is a **fruit**."],
  ["is rhubarb a fruit or vegetable | what is rhubarb", "**Rhubarb** is botanically a **vegetable** — we eat its stalks — but it's usually cooked with sugar like a fruit in pies and crumbles. Its leaves are poisonous, so only eat the stalks."],
  ["is a pumpkin a fruit | pumpkin facts", "Yes, a **pumpkin** is a fruit — a type of squash in the gourd family, which also includes cucumbers and melons. Pumpkins are native to North America and can grow to over 2,000 pounds in giant-pumpkin contests!"],
  ["where do bananas come from | how do bananas grow | banana facts", "Bananas grow in bunches on large plants (not true trees) in tropical regions. They were first domesticated in Southeast Asia and New Guinea. Most bananas sold worldwide are the **Cavendish** variety, and their seeds are tiny and sterile."],
  ["how do pineapples grow | where do pineapples come from | pineapple facts", "A **pineapple** grows from the center of a low, spiky plant — one fruit per plant at a time, taking about 18–24 months! Pineapples are native to South America. You can even grow a new plant from a pineapple's leafy top."],
  ["what is a pomegranate | pomegranate | how to eat a pomegranate", "A **pomegranate** is a round fruit full of hundreds of juicy red seeds called **arils**. To get them out easily, cut it in half and tap the back with a spoon over a bowl, or break it apart underwater so the arils sink and the white pith floats."],
  ["what is a dragon fruit | dragon fruit | pitaya", "**Dragon fruit** (pitaya) is the fruit of a cactus. It has bright pink or yellow skin with green scales and white or magenta flesh dotted with tiny black seeds. It tastes mildly sweet, like a cross between a kiwi and a pear."],
  ["what is a durian | durian | why does durian smell", "**Durian** is a large, spiky Southeast Asian fruit known as the 'king of fruits'. Its creamy flesh tastes sweet and custardy, but its smell is so strong that it's banned on some public transport and in some hotels in Singapore."],
  ["what is a jackfruit | jackfruit", "**Jackfruit** is the largest tree fruit in the world, sometimes weighing over 80 pounds (35 kg). Ripe jackfruit tastes sweet and tropical; young green jackfruit has a stringy texture and is used as a plant-based meat substitute."],
  ["what is a kiwi fruit | kiwi | where do kiwis come from", "**Kiwifruit** has fuzzy brown skin and bright green flesh with tiny black seeds. It originally comes from China, where it was called the Chinese gooseberry, and was renamed after New Zealand's kiwi bird when New Zealand began exporting it. You can even eat the skin!"],
  ["what is a lychee | lychee", "A **lychee** is a small tropical fruit from southern China with bumpy pink-red skin and sweet, juicy, translucent white flesh around a big brown seed. Peel it and don't eat the seed."],
  ["what is a papaya | papaya", "A **papaya** is a tropical fruit native to Central America and southern Mexico, with orange flesh and lots of black seeds in the middle. It contains **papain**, an enzyme used to tenderize meat."],
  ["what is a plantain | plantain vs banana", "A **plantain** is a starchy cousin of the banana that's usually **cooked** before eating. Green plantains are savory (great for tostones), while ripe black ones are sweet (great for maduros). They're a staple in Africa, the Caribbean and Latin America."],
  ["what is a persimmon | persimmon", "A **persimmon** is an orange fruit that looks a bit like a tomato. **Fuyu** persimmons are squat and can be eaten firm like an apple; **Hachiya** persimmons are acorn-shaped and must be very soft and ripe, or they taste unpleasantly dry and puckery."],
  ["what is a fig | figs | fig facts", "A **fig** is a soft, sweet fruit with a chewy skin and lots of tiny seeds inside. Technically, a fig is an inside-out cluster of flowers! Figs were one of the first plants farmed by humans."],
  ["what is a date fruit | dates | where do dates come from", "**Dates** are the sweet, sticky fruit of the date palm tree, grown in hot, dry places like the Middle East and North Africa. They've been cultivated for thousands of years and are traditionally eaten to break the fast during Ramadan."],
  ["what is an olive | why are olives bitter | green vs black olives", "**Olives** are the fruit of the olive tree. Fresh olives are extremely bitter, so they're cured in brine, salt or lye before eating. **Green olives** are picked unripe; **black olives** are riper (or turned black by processing)."],
  ["what is a coconut | is a coconut a nut | coconut facts", "A **coconut** isn't really a nut — botanically it's a **drupe**, like a peach or cherry, with a hard shell around the seed. Young green coconuts are full of coconut water; mature brown ones have thick white meat used to make coconut milk."],
  ["what is avocado | avocado facts | where do avocados come from", "**Avocados** are fruits from trees native to Mexico and Central America. They're rich in healthy fats and are ripe when they yield slightly to gentle pressure. The word comes from the Aztec (Nahuatl) word 'ahuacatl'."],
  ["how many kinds of apples are there | apple varieties | apple facts", "There are over **7,500 apple varieties** grown worldwide. Apples originally came from the mountains of Kazakhstan in Central Asia. Popular varieties include Gala, Fuji, Honeycrisp, Granny Smith and McIntosh."],
  ["what is the difference between oranges and tangerines | mandarin vs orange | clementine vs tangerine", "**Mandarins** are small, easy-to-peel citrus fruits. **Tangerines** and **clementines** are types of mandarin; clementines are usually seedless and very sweet. **Oranges** are larger, with thicker skin that's harder to peel."],
  ["what is a grapefruit | grapefruit facts", "A **grapefruit** is a large, tangy citrus fruit that first appeared in Barbados in the 1700s as a natural cross between the pomelo and the sweet orange. It's named for growing in clusters, like grapes. It can interfere with some medicines, so check with a doctor if you take any."],
  ["what are the different types of potatoes | waxy vs starchy potatoes | best potato for mashing", "**Starchy** potatoes (like Russet) are fluffy — great for baking, mashing and fries. **Waxy** potatoes (like red or fingerling) hold their shape — great for potato salad and roasting. **All-purpose** potatoes (like Yukon Gold) are in between."],
  ["where do potatoes come from | potato history | potato facts", "**Potatoes** were first domesticated in the Andes Mountains of Peru and Bolivia at least 7,000 years ago. Spanish explorers brought them to Europe in the 1500s. Peru still grows thousands of potato varieties."],
  ["where do tomatoes come from | tomato history | tomato facts", "**Tomatoes** are native to western South America and were domesticated in Mexico. They reached Europe in the 1500s, where some people at first believed they were poisonous. Today Italy, with its tomato sauces, is famous for them."],
  ["what is broccoli | broccoli facts | broccoli cauliflower cabbage same plant", "**Broccoli** is the flower head of a plant in the cabbage family. Amazingly, broccoli, cauliflower, cabbage, kale, Brussels sprouts and kohlrabi were all bred from the **same wild mustard plant** (*Brassica oleracea*)."],
  ["why are carrots orange | were carrots always orange", "Early cultivated carrots were mostly **purple and yellow**. Orange carrots were developed by farmers in the Netherlands in the 1600s. Their orange color comes from **beta-carotene**, which your body turns into vitamin A."],
  ["carrots and eyesight", "Carrots contain beta-carotene, which your body uses to make vitamin A — important for healthy eyes. But they won't give you night vision! That myth was spread by the British in World War II to hide their use of new radar technology."],
  ["what is a leek | leeks", "A **leek** is a member of the onion family with long white and pale green stalks and a mild, sweet onion flavor. Dirt often hides between its layers, so slice and rinse it well. It's the national symbol of Wales!"],
  ["what is a shallot | shallot vs onion", "A **shallot** is a small, mild member of the onion family with a delicate, slightly sweet flavor and a hint of garlic. Shallots grow in clusters and are popular in French cooking and dressings."],
  ["what is eggplant | aubergine | eggplant facts", "**Eggplant** (called aubergine in the UK) is a purple fruit in the nightshade family, related to tomatoes and potatoes. Its name comes from early white varieties that looked like eggs. It's used in dishes like baba ghanoush, moussaka and ratatouille."],
  ["what is zucchini | courgette | zucchini facts", "**Zucchini** (courgette in the UK) is a summer squash. It grows very fast — sometimes inches in a day — and its yellow flowers are edible too. It's great grilled, roasted, in bread or spiralized into 'zoodles'."],
  ["what is okra | okra", "**Okra** is a green seed pod that came from Africa. When cooked, it releases a thick, slimy substance that's great for thickening stews like gumbo. Roasting or frying keeps it crispier."],
  ["what is cassava | cassava | yuca", "**Cassava** (yuca) is a starchy root from South America that's now a staple for hundreds of millions of people in Africa, Asia and Latin America. It must be peeled and cooked because raw cassava contains toxic compounds. Tapioca is made from cassava starch."],
  ["what is taro | taro root", "**Taro** is a starchy root vegetable grown in tropical regions. It's used in Hawaiian poi, Chinese dim sum, and purple taro bubble tea. Like cassava, it must be cooked before eating."],
  ["what is kale | kale facts", "**Kale** is a leafy green in the cabbage family. It's packed with vitamins A, C and K. Massaging raw kale with a little oil and salt makes it softer for salads."],
  ["what is spinach | does spinach make you strong | spinach iron myth", "**Spinach** is a leafy green rich in vitamins and minerals. It does contain iron, but a long-told story that it has huge amounts came from an exaggeration — it's healthy, but not Popeye-level magic!"],
  ["is a mushroom a vegetable", "A **mushroom** isn't a plant or a vegetable — it's the fruiting body of a **fungus**. Mushrooms are cooked like vegetables and add rich umami flavor. Never eat wild mushrooms unless an expert identifies them — some are deadly."],
  ["what is seaweed | can you eat seaweed | types of edible seaweed", "**Seaweed** is a type of marine algae. Edible kinds include **nori** (for sushi), **kombu** (for dashi broth), **wakame** (in miso soup) and **dulse**. It's rich in minerals like iodine."],
  ["what are legumes | what are pulses | examples of legumes", "**Legumes** are plants whose seeds grow in pods, such as beans, peas, lentils, chickpeas and peanuts. The dried seeds are called **pulses**. They're a great source of protein and fiber."],
  ["examples of whole grains", "**Whole grains** keep all three parts of the grain: bran, germ and endosperm, so they have more fiber and nutrients. Examples are oats, brown rice, quinoa, whole wheat, barley and popcorn. **Refined** grains like white flour have the bran and germ removed."],
  ["what is rice | how many types of rice are there | where does rice come from", "**Rice** is the seed of a grass, first farmed in China about 9,000 years ago. There are thousands of varieties, grouped as **long-grain** (basmati, jasmine — fluffy), **medium-grain** and **short-grain** (sushi rice, arborio — sticky). It feeds more than half the world's people."],
  ["what is wheat | how is flour made | where does flour come from", "**Wheat** is a grass whose seeds are ground into **flour** in a mill. White flour uses just the starchy endosperm; whole wheat flour includes the bran and germ too. Wheat was first farmed in the Fertile Crescent of the Middle East about 10,000 years ago."],
  ["where does chocolate come from | how is chocolate made | what is chocolate made from", "Chocolate comes from **cacao beans**, the seeds of the cacao tree, native to Central and South America. The beans are scooped from pods, **fermented**, dried, roasted and ground into cocoa mass. That's mixed with sugar (and cocoa butter and milk) to make chocolate. Today, Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana grow most of the world's cacao."],
  ["who invented chocolate | history of chocolate", "The Olmec, Maya and Aztec peoples of Mesoamerica made bitter cacao drinks thousands of years ago — the Aztecs even used cacao beans as money! Spanish explorers brought cacao to Europe in the 1500s, where sugar was added. The first solid chocolate bar was made in England in 1847."],
  ["what is white chocolate | is white chocolate real chocolate", "**White chocolate** is made from **cocoa butter**, sugar and milk, but no cocoa solids — that's why it's pale and doesn't taste like dark chocolate. Some people say it isn't 'real' chocolate, but it does come from the cacao bean."],
  ["where does coffee come from | how is coffee made | coffee history", "**Coffee** comes from the seeds (called beans) inside red coffee cherries that grow on shrubs. Legend says it was discovered in Ethiopia when a goat herder noticed his goats getting energetic after eating the cherries. The seeds are dried, roasted and ground, then brewed with hot water. Brazil grows the most coffee today."],
  ["where does tea come from | how is tea made | tea history", "True **tea** comes from the leaves of the *Camellia sinensis* plant, first used in China thousands of years ago. Green, black, white and oolong teas all come from the same plant — they're processed differently. Herbal 'teas' like chamomile and peppermint aren't from the tea plant at all."],
  ["where does sugar come from | how is sugar made", "Most **sugar** comes from **sugarcane** (a tall tropical grass first domesticated in New Guinea) or **sugar beets** (a root grown in cooler places). The juice is extracted, boiled down and crystallized. Brazil and India are the biggest sugarcane producers."],
  ["how is cheese made | where does cheese come from | cheese making", "Cheese is made by:\n1. Warming milk and adding a culture of good bacteria.\n2. Adding **rennet** (an enzyme) to make the milk curdle into solid **curds** and liquid **whey**.\n3. Cutting, draining and pressing the curds, and salting them.\n4. Aging the cheese anywhere from days to years. Different milks, bacteria and aging times create thousands of cheeses."],
  ["how is pasta made | how is dried pasta made", "Dried pasta is made by mixing **durum wheat semolina** with water into a stiff dough, pushing it through metal dies to form shapes (this is called **extrusion**), and drying it slowly. Fresh pasta, often made with eggs, is rolled and cut by hand or machine."],
  ["where does vanilla flavoring come from | is vanilla from beavers | what is vanillin", "Pure **vanilla extract** is made by soaking cured vanilla bean pods in alcohol. Most cheaper 'vanilla flavor' uses **vanillin** made from other plant sources. The story about beaver-gland vanilla (castoreum) is mostly a myth today — it's very rarely used because it's rare and expensive."],
  ["how is maple syrup made | where does maple syrup come from", "**Maple syrup** is made from the sap of sugar maple trees, collected in late winter and early spring. The thin, watery sap is boiled down for hours — it takes about **40 gallons of sap to make 1 gallon of syrup** (a 40-to-1 ratio). Quebec, Canada makes most of the world's maple syrup."],
  ["where does honey come from", "**Honey** is made by honeybees. They collect flower **nectar**, add enzymes in their bodies, and store it in wax honeycomb cells. Fanning their wings evaporates water until it thickens into honey. One bee makes only about 1/12 of a teaspoon in its whole life!"],
  ["how is olive oil made | what is extra virgin olive oil", "**Olive oil** is made by crushing whole olives into a paste and separating the oil from the water and solids. **Extra virgin** olive oil is the highest grade — made only by mechanical pressing without heat or chemicals, with low acidity and great flavor."],
  ["how is salt made | where does salt come from", "Salt comes from two main sources: **sea salt** is made by evaporating seawater in shallow ponds, and **rock salt** is mined from underground deposits left by ancient seas. Table salt is usually refined and often has added iodine."],
  ["how is yogurt made | what is yogurt", "**Yogurt** is milk fermented by friendly bacteria (like *Lactobacillus bulgaricus* and *Streptococcus thermophilus*). The bacteria turn milk sugar (lactose) into lactic acid, which thickens the milk and makes it tangy."],
  ["how are tortilla chips made | who invented potato chips", "**Tortilla chips** are corn tortillas cut into wedges and fried or baked. **Potato chips** are often credited to chef George Crum in Saratoga Springs, New York, in 1853, though the story may be partly legend — similar recipes appeared in earlier cookbooks."],
  ["how is ketchup made | where did ketchup come from", "Today's **ketchup** is made from tomatoes, vinegar, sugar, salt and spices. The word likely comes from a fermented fish sauce from southern China and Southeast Asia (kê-tsiap). Tomato ketchup became popular in the US in the 1800s."],
  ["where do peanuts come from | how is peanut butter made", "Peanuts were first grown in South America thousands of years ago. **Peanut butter** is made by roasting shelled peanuts and grinding them into a paste, often with salt (and sometimes sugar and oil). George Washington Carver promoted many uses of peanuts."],
  ["spice trade history", "The **spice trade** was a network of routes moving spices like pepper, cinnamon, cloves and nutmeg from Asia to the Middle East, Africa and Europe for thousands of years. Spices were so valuable that the search for faster routes helped launch European voyages of exploration in the 1400s–1500s."],
  ["what is the columbian exchange | what foods came from the americas", "The **Columbian Exchange** was the transfer of plants, animals and foods between the Americas and the rest of the world after 1492. Foods from the Americas include **tomatoes, potatoes, corn, chocolate, vanilla, chili peppers, peanuts, pineapples, avocados and turkey**. Wheat, rice, sugar, coffee, cattle and pigs went the other way."],
  ["where do noodles come from | oldest noodles | who invented noodles", "**Noodles** have a very long history in China. Archaeologists found a 4,000-year-old bowl of millet noodles at the Lajia site in China. Today noodles are eaten worldwide, from Japanese ramen and udon to Italian pasta."],
  ["where did ice cream come from | history of ice cream | who invented ice cream", "Frozen treats go back thousands of years — ancient Persians and Chinese mixed snow or ice with fruit and flavorings. Ice cream as we know it developed in Italy and France in the 1600s and 1700s and became hugely popular in the US in the 1800s."],
  ["where did sandwiches come from | who invented the sandwich", "The **sandwich** is named after John Montagu, the **4th Earl of Sandwich** in 1700s England, who supposedly asked for meat between bread so he could eat without stopping what he was doing. People had eaten food on bread long before that, though!"],
  ["where did french fries come from | are french fries from france", "**French fries** were probably invented in **Belgium** or **France** in the late 1700s or 1800s — both countries claim them. One theory says American soldiers in World War I called them 'French' fries because the Belgian soldiers spoke French."],
  ["what is a cacao pod | cacao vs cocoa", "A **cacao pod** is the football-shaped fruit of the cacao tree, holding 20–60 seeds wrapped in sweet white pulp. 'Cacao' usually refers to the plant and raw beans; 'cocoa' to the roasted, processed products like cocoa powder."],
  ["how are cashews grown | where do cashews come from", "**Cashews** grow at the bottom of a fruit called the **cashew apple**, on trees native to Brazil. The nut's shell contains an irritating oil (related to poison ivy), so cashews must be roasted or steamed and shelled carefully — that's why you never see them sold in the shell."],
  ["how do almonds grow | where do almonds come from", "**Almonds** are the seeds of a fruit related to peaches and cherries. The fruit's fuzzy green hull splits open when ripe, revealing the shell with the almond inside. California grows most of the world's almonds."]
);
